INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4075,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11931241','An Evaluation of First-in-Human Studies for RNA Oligonucleotides.','Stern S, Wange RL, Rogers H.','2024','crick sirnas untreatable crick charactered sirnas expediting sirnas risc crick immunostimulation steric ribosome sirnas dicer risc inds endonucleases exonucleases physiochemical exponents exponents exponents phosphorothioate physiochemical fivefold exponents nanavati galnac nanavati galnac galnac sirnas informatic search360 inds immunostimulators intracerebroventricular eteplirsen sirnas galnac sirnas galnac phosphorodiamidate morpholino intrathecally intravitreally intrathecally galnac galnac galnac galnac intrathecally phosphorothioate morpholino sirnas fluoronation conjugations galnac noaels ood rug dministration pproved ligonucleotide ased herapies osing trategy fomivirsen ivt ivt q2w q4w mipomersen thrice eteplirsen pmo skipping q7d nusinersen q2w q4m patisiran attr risc q3m inotersen attr golodirsen pmo skipping givosiran porphyria galnac risc q3m q4w viltolarsen pmo skipping lumasiran galnac risc q3m casimersen pmo skipping inclisiran hefh q2w vutrisiran attr galnac risc q3m tofersen eplontersen transthyretin q4w nedosiran galnac risc q3m q4m schematics hnv inds icv intracerebroventricular ivt roa exponents allometry exponents exponents chemistries overutilization schuck pacanowski dasaradhi mohmmed blau crooke scoles minikel pulst e323 adeniyi ramamoorthy s3a methoxyethyl e218 theil xenobiotica nanavati mcmullen galnac3','Abstract Most oligonucleotide therapeutics use Watson–Crick–Franklin base-pairing hybridization to target RNA and mitigate disease-related protein production. Using targets that were previously inaccessible to small molecules and biologics, synthetic nucleotides have provided treatments for severely debilitating and life-threatening diseases. However, these therapeutics possess unique pharmacologies that require specific considerations for their distribution, clearance, and other clinical pharmacology characteristics. Namely, one hurdle in the drug development of these therapeutics remains the prediction of human dose that results in exposures comparable with or below those seen at no observed adverse effect level in animals. For first-in-human (FIH) clinical trials, this often involves allometric scaling based on body surface area (BSA) or body weight (BW). In this study, we reviewed the current literature and surveyed elements across 16 approved oligonucleotide therapeutic New Drug Applications approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration in the period from September 1998 to January 2024, and 89 Investigational New Drug (IND) programs with available FIH clinical trials conducted from January 2015 to January 2024, to understand dose selection in early-stage development of oligonucleotide therapeutics. The surveyed elements across these programs include study design, route of administration, dosing regimen, interspecies scaling approach, and the most sensitive species. Of 89 IND programs and 16 approved therapeutics, intravenous and subcutaneous were the most common route of administration, no observable adverse event levels were frequently derived from nonhuman primates, BSA and BW were adjusted for in similar frequencies, patients were predominantly enrolled in FIH trials, and the most common design was a single or multiple ascending dose trial. Keywords: oligonucleotide, drug development, antisense oligonucleotide, siRNA Introduction Oligonucleotide therapeutics such as antisense oligonucleotides (ASOs), splice-switching oligonucleotides, and small interfering RNAs (siRNAs) have opened the door to treating severely debilitating and life-threatening genetic diseases that were previously deemed untreatable. Oligonucleotide therapeutics are now also being utilized to treat more prevalent diseases. Most oligonucleotide therapeutics utilize complementary base-pairing to target the source of pathogenesis, exploiting Watson–Crick–Franklin hybridization to modulate target RNA function and specific gene expression through various mechanisms. 1 Advancement in chemical modifications has led to improvements in structural properties, promoting favorable pharmacokinetics, metabolic stability, and high target affinity of oligonucleotide therapeutics. Currently, 16 oligonucleotide therapeutics have been approved for 12 diseases by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The majority of these were approved in the last decade. Additional advancements have been made in the design and execution of clinical trials using oligonucleotide therapies. For example, the few well-charactered ASOs and siRNAs have paved the way for others within the same chemical class to be developed. However, several challenges, such as dose selection, remain in early-stage development of these therapeutics. Thus, we sought to understand first-in-human (FIH) dosing of oligonucleotide therapeutics and the clinical trial design elements, notably the interspecies scaling approaches, by conducting an initial literature review and subsequently an analysis of FDA-approved products and investigational drug development programs with original data. We outline some lessons learned from the design and development of approved and investigational oligonucleotide therapeutics, which may provide thoughtful insight into FIH clinical trial design, interspecies/dose scaling approaches, and dose and dosing interval selection may shed light on best practices for transitioning into FIH trials, expediting the lengthy process of moving therapeutics from preclinical to clinical settings. Mechanisms of action The major mechanisms for oligonucleotide therapeutics are as follows: (1) single-stranded ASOs alter gene expression via complementary base pairing and recruitment of RNase H for the initiation of target RNA degradation, (2) splice-altering single-stranded oligonucleotides direct pre-mRNA splicing via blocking access to the transcripts, or (3) double-stranded siRNAs, which guide the strand to assemble the RNA-induced silencing complex (RISC) and subsequently lead to mRNA degradation ( Fig. 1 ). 2 – 4 Indirect mechanisms not involving Watson–Crick–Franklin hybridization (e.g., immunostimulation) are outside the scope of this article. Importantly, antisense mechanisms can differ based on cellular localization, whether inside the nucleus or within the cytoplasm. For instance, oligonucleotides that exert splicing site modulation act within the nucleus, whereas ASOs recruiting RNAse H can operate within the nucleus or cytoplasm. 3 , 4 Chemical modifications of an oligonucleotide can enhance pairing stability and metabolic stability, alter protein binding, and have other effects, impacting potency, localization, and distribution. These modifications can influence the characterization of these oligonucleotides and impact safety and efficacy. FIG. 1. Open in a new tab Comparison of the mechanism behind ASO and siRNA. ASOs are single-stranded oligonucleotides that bind to their mRNA target leading to steric blocking of the ribosome and the subsequent translation, degradation of mRNA via RNase H, or modulation of splicing of pre-mRNA. siRNAs are double-stranded and dicer removes the passenger strand as the guided strand enters the RISC complex and subsequently to the complementary mRNA to be cleaved. Created by Biorender.com . ASO, antisense oligonucleotide. Dosing considerations FIH clinical trials are often conducted using patients within the intended population instead of healthy subjects. In addition, oligonucleotide therapeutics tend to have long pharmacodynamic half-lives stemming from sustained target knockdown, which introduce novel challenges for FIH dose selection. For instance, Rogers and colleagues (2021) evaluated 152 clinical studies from 14 Investigational New Drugs (INDs) and 7 New Drug Applications to determine that only 20% (30/152) of studies included healthy subjects, the majority of which were in Phase I trials, compared with the 80% of studies conducted with patients. 5 In addition, nearly 44% of FIH trials involved single-dose administration, 38% included multiple-dose regimens, and 18% included a combination of single-dose and multiple-dose regimens. Interestingly, 67% (32/48) of FIH trials and 64% (97/152) of all the oligonucleotide therapeutic clinical trials used fixed doses rather than body weight (BW) dosing. Many of the same principles and approaches are applicable to designing FIH trials with patients, as described in the Guidance for Industry titled “Estimating the Maximum Safe Starting Dose in Initial Clinical Trials for Therapeutics in Adult Healthy Volunteers.” The concentration of the drug that results in no observable adverse event level (NOAEL) for each species tested should be identified and converted to the human equivalent dose (HED) using scaling factors. It is recommended that for systemically administered therapeutics, the conversion should be normalized to body surface area (BSA) instead of BW. Often the starting dose is derived from the NOAEL from the most sensitive species (that which generates the lowest HED) by applying an appropriate safety factor (typically 10-fold) to the NOAEL dose. If there is a basis for concluding a particular species is more relevant to the toxicity assessment (e.g., only one species is pharmacologically responsive), the FIH dose may be calculated from the NOAEL dose observed in this species. Dose selection and adequacy of interspecies scaling approach Given the distinct and unique pharmacokinetic profiles of oligonucleotide therapeutics, including their extensive distribution within tissues, relatively long tissue half-life, and degradation by endonucleases and exonucleases, physiochemical properties, and chemical modifications, the dose scaling approaches used for small molecules or biologics are often not directly applicable. We searched the literature to identify trends in FIH dose selection and interspecies scaling approaches using search terms “antisense oligonucleotide,” “oligonucleotide,” “siRNA,” in combination with “scaling approaches,” “body surface area,” “adjusted by body weight,” or “first in human dosing.” Prediction of human plasma exposure (area under the curve, AUC) derived from animal models intended for toxicological studies determined by comparing plasma AUC for each species. 6 Early literature suggests that human pharmacokinetic parameters for oligonucleotides can be predicted with less than 50% error if at least three species are included, with mouse and nonhuman primates (NHPs) being the most prevalent species. 7 However, when three animal species were not available, the use of allometric scaling with fixed exponents provided similar predictions in two sensitive species when the chemical class was well-characterized, except for clearance, which cannot be determined a priori . 7 The use of fixed exponents led to substantial discrepancies in prediction particularly because of the wide range of exponents calculated. Yu and colleagues (2015) observed that the HED of unconjugated phosphorothioate-backbone ASOs and the subsequent systemic exposure were accurately predicted using BW-based scaling approaches derived from mice or NHPs following single and repeat parenteral administration of nine ASOs with 2’-MOE modifications, 20-to-21 nucleotides in length, and similar characteristics such as charge, molecular weight, and physiochemical properties. 8 Following multiple dose levels from low (3–12 mg/kg) to high (20–40 mg/kg) administered both subcutaneously and intravenously, NHPs accurately predicted human plasma AUC for ASOs, with the exposure ratio within twofold after adjusting for BW. However, when adjusting for BW, the plasma exposure ratio in mice is nearly fivefold lower than in humans, necessitating a multiplication factor of five to provide a more accurate prediction of AUC in humans. Interspecies differences were pronounced when scaling ASO plasma concentration in mice to humans, requiring a median scaling factor in a majority of cases (range from 5- to 11-fold). 8 The prediction of human clearance and exposure from preclinical studies enables FIH dose selection and is vital to the early success of a drug development program. Historically, pharmacokinetic parameter predictions used BW-based scaling approaches, particularly for small-molecule drugs, antibodies, and antibody–drug conjugates. 9 – 11 Small-molecule drugs and large proteins use scaling exponents such as 0.75 to 1 with reasonable reliability. However, given the unique features of oligonucleotide metabolism, oligonucleotide therapeutics intended for systemic circulation present challenges with interspecies scaling. Nanavati and colleagues (2021) demonstrated that the dose of conjugated and unconjugated ASOs normalized to BW (mg/kg) was a reasonable scaling approach from NHP to human with a C trough ratio close to 1 (0.96–1.7) compared with BSA (mg/m 2 ) following 1-h intravenous and subcutaneous injections of ASOs administered every other day for the first week followed by once every fourth day or once weekly at doses ranging from 1 to 4 mg/kg. 12 Notably, the prediction of C trough in NHP to human normalized by BW-based dose for unconjugated and GalNAc-conjugated ASOs demonstrated correlation ( R 2 = 0.64) with a slope of regression near 1, supporting that BW-based scaling may provide direct prediction from NHP C trough to human C trough . Nanavati et al. (2021) demonstrated that GalNAc-conjugated ASOs have higher plasma clearance compared with unconjugated ASOs due to the hepatic uptake and distribution in NHP with 1:1 BW scaling relationship to humans. 12 The 1:1 BW scaling approach led to reliable predictions, whereas BSA-based scaling led to under prediction of the clearance with a clearance ratio from NHP to humans of 0.33 and 0.37 for unconjugated and conjugated ASOs, respectively. 12 Thus, scaling reliability is likely directly related to both physicochemical characteristics and species-dependent considerations. While data exist for scaling approaches with GalNAc-conjugated ASOs and other unconjugated oligonucleotides, limited data have been published in relation to siRNAs. Further research investigating the similarities and differences between ASOs and siRNA in terms of pharmacokinetic profiles and scaling approaches is warranted. Overall, there is no consensus in the limited literature regarding the most accurate scaling approach. Thus, to illuminate the FIH dosing considerations for oligonucleotide therapeutics, we sought to review approved oligonucleotide therapeutics by the U.S. FDA, Center for Drug Evaluation and Research, and selected investigational drug programs to understand the real-world utility of each scaling approach and report trends in study design to streamline their development. Methods ASO and siRNA drug development programs were identified through an internal FDA informatic platform known as Search360. Search terms included the following: “antisense oligonucleotide,” “oligonucleotide,” and “siRNA.” Drug development programs that were retrieved through this search were manually reviewed to identify programs that conducted their FIH trial between January 1, 2015, and January 1, 2024. Drug development programs were only included if the drug program was considered a commercial application and active, excluding active research INDs, withdrawn, terminated, and inactive commercial programs. Drug development programs were excluded if the oligonucleotide included non-RNA-binding targets, such as immunostimulators (e.g., toll-like receptor agonists) and aptamers. In addition, drug development programs were excluded if their FIH trial had no existing protocol (e.g., when the FIH trial was conducted outside the United States, and therefore, the protocol and data are not submitted). All CDER-approved RNA-oligonucleotide therapeutics from September 1998 (i.e., first approved RNA oligonucleotide) to January 2024 were also included. For IND programs, protocols for the FIH trial, amendments, and general IND plans were obtained. For approved programs, FIH protocols, labeling at the time of approval, amendments, common technical documents, clinical pharmacology review documents, and integrated reviews were obtained. Documents were reviewed to describe the study population (e.g., healthy normal volunteers vs. patient population), number of participants enrolled, trial design, trial duration, dosing regimen, randomization scheme, route of administration, open-label extension or follow-up, scaling approaches adjusted by BW versus BSA, NOAEL, and target engagement and pharmacodynamic assays. Trial design was defined as a single ascending dose (SAD) study if the study subjects received a single administration of study drug with ascending dose cohorts, as a multiple ascending dose (MAD) study if trial subjects received more than one dose of the study drug in increasing doses, as a maximal tolerated dose (MTD) study if trial subjects received increasing doses until maximum tolerability was determined, or as other if the trial subjects received any other design besides an SAD or MAD study (e.g., randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled study). Current Landscape of Dose Selection in Approved and Investigational Nucleotide-Based Therapeutic Program In this assessment, a total of 221 IND programs with distinct oligonucleotide products and 16 FDA-approved drug programs were identified. Eighty-nine IND programs and all 16 approved drug programs were selected based on the aforementioned criteria. Out of 89 IND programs with FIH clinical trial protocols available, 62.9% (56/89) of the FIH clinical trials were conducted in the United States. Of the 33 FIH clinical trials that were not conducted in the United States, the majority were conducted in Canada, Australia, the United Kingdom, and/or Europe. Of the 89 IND programs, 33.6% (30/89) of FIH clinical trials used SAD designs, 27.0% (24/89) of FIH clinical trials used MAD designs, and 30.3% (27/89) used an SAD/MAD study design. For the remaining programs, 4.5% (4/89) of FIH clinical trials used MTD designs, 3.4% (3/89) used a seamless design, including a dose-finding phase and a randomized, double-blind phase, and 1.2% (1/89) of FIH clinical trials used a single-dose range design. All SAD and/or MAD FIH trial designs included a stepwise dose escalation based on nonclinical data. Thirty-one out of 89 FIH clinical trials enrolled healthy normal subjects. Of the 31 FIH clinical trials that enrolled healthy subjects, 15.6% (5/31) enrolled both healthy subjects and patients in different cohorts. For instance, healthy subjects were enrolled in 70% (21/30) of the SAD design studies, 4.2% (1/24) of the MAD design studies, and 44.4% (12/27) of the SAD/MAD studies. Randomization was used in 70.8% (63/89) of FIH clinical trials, with a 6:2 active:placebo ratio being the most common randomization scheme. Approximately 60.7% (54/89) of the FIH clinical trials used a sentinel dose cohort with 2 subjects randomized 1:1 to receive the active drug or placebo. All developmental programs measured clinical endpoints or biomarker endpoints as part of their FIH trial design. Of the approved oligonucleotide products, 81.3% (13/16) of FIH clinical trials were MAD study designs 12.5% (2/16) of FIH clinical trials were SAD designs, and 6.2% (1/16) were SAD/MAD study design. Out of the 16 approved drug programs, 62.5% (10/16) of the FIH clinical trials were conducted in the United States. Eight of the 16 approved drug programs of the FIH clinical trials enrolled healthy normal volunteers, while the 62.5% (10/16) other FIH clinical trials enrolled patients in the intended patient population. The investigational and approved oligonucleotide therapeutics were summarized by target indication area ( Fig. 2 ). For instance, of the 89 oligonucleotide programs under development, 41% were being developed for a neurology indication, 16% for an oncology indication, and 12% for cardiology and cardiometabolic/endocrinology indications. Of the 16 approved oligonucleotide products, 62.5% have been approved for neurology indications and 25% have been approved for cardiology or cardiometabolic/endocrinology indications such as primary hyperlipidemia, familial hypercholesterolemia, and primary hyperoxaluria type 1. FIG. 2. Open in a new tab Oligonucleotide drug development programs and indication area. ( A ) The distribution of indication areas is shown for the investigational oligonucleotide programs. ( B ) The distribution of the approved oligonucleotide products is shown. Route of administration considerations Of the 89 investigational drug development programs, the most common route of administration was subcutaneous (37.1%, 33/89), followed by intravenous (26.7%, 24/89), intrathecal (24.4%, 22/89), intravitreal (5.6%, 5/89), and inhaled (2/89), intracerebroventricular (1/89), cutaneous (1/89), or a combination of subcutaneous, intramuscular, and intravenous (1/89). Out of the 16 approved oligonucleotide therapies, the following routes of administration were used in the FIH clinical trial: 43.8% (7/16) intravenous; 37.5% (6/16) subcutaneous, 12.5% (2/16) intrathecal, and 6.3% (1/16) intravitreal. Their FIH clinical trial was the approved route of administration 100% of the time. One program, eteplirsen, included the approved route of administration as well as intramuscular injection. Of the approved oligonucleotides, 5 of the 6 siRNAs are GalNAc conjugated and administered subcutaneously. Of the oligonucleotide therapies under investigation, 24 out of 33 siRNAs are conjugated (e.g., GalNAc, lipid moiety, monoclonal antibody) and the majority (66.7%, 22/33) of siRNA products are administered subcutaneously. Four out of the 16 approved oligonucleotide products and 8 of the 89 are phosphorodiamidate morpholino oligonucleotides and all are administered intravenously. Four of the 89 are micro-RNAs and 3 out of the 4 are administered intravenously. Lastly, of the approved oligonucleotides, 37.5% (6/16) were ASOs, 2 ASOs were administered intrathecally, 3 administered subcutaneously, and 1 was administered intravitreally. Of the investigational programs, 49.4% (44/89) of the oligonucleotides under investigation were considered ASOs, 41% (18/44) were administered intrathecally, and 27.3% (12/44) were administered subcutaneously. NOAEL and FIH dosing Seventeen of the 57 IND development programs with subcutaneous or intravenous administration utilized BSA-based scaling approach from nonclinical data to obtain a human equivalent starting dose with a safety factor. Ten of the 57 drug development programs utilized BW-based scaling. The remainder, 30/57, did not include a specified adjustment for scaling based on BW or BSA in their rationale for their starting dose. Specifically, all GalNAc conjugates were administered subcutaneously, 57.7% (15/26) of the GalNAc conjugates were conjugated to siRNA, and of those investigational programs that included scaling approaches in FIH clinical trial protocols, 9 GalNAc conjugates used scaling adjusted by BSA and 8 GalNAc conjugates adjusted by BW. Intrathecally administered oligonucleotide therapeutics were normalized for cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) volume from the sensitive species, monkeys in most cases, to humans. All but six out of 89 drug development programs included NOAEL data in support of their FIH clinical trial protocol. The most common chemical classes from the drug development programs included single-stranded phosphorothioate-backbone ASOs, single-stranded morpholino-backbone ASOs, and double-stranded siRNAs. In addition, these products had various substitutions (e.g., 2′MOE, 2′-fluoronation) and conjugations (e.g., GalNAc, lipid nanoparticle). Ten FIH protocols out of 89 did not specify the sensitive species from which the NOAEL was determined. In 75.9% (60/79) of FIH drug development programs, the sensitive species was NHP, followed by rodents (13/79), both rodent and NHP (5/79), and hamster (1/79). Of the 49 oligonucleotide therapeutics administered via subcutaneous and intravenous route, 67.3% (33/49) of investigational programs used NHPs as the most sensitive species, 24.5% (12/49) used rodents, and 8.2% (4/49) used both rodents and NHPs as the sensitive species. Of the approved oligonucleotides, 2 out of 16 approved oligonucleotide products utilized BSA for their human equivalent starting dose in their FIH clinical trial. Four out of 16 used BW-based scaling, 2 out of 16 were intrathecal administration and normalized to CSF volume, and 1 oligonucleotide product used intravitreal administration. Seven out of 16 of the approved oligonucleotide products did not include BW or BSA adjustments for scaling approach as part of their FIH clinical trial protocol. Target engagement and pharmacodynamic biomarkers Pharmacodynamic biomarkers can be leveraged to display target engagement such as target knockdown and support dose escalation. Sixty-seven out of 89 drug development programs utilized a pharmacodynamic biomarker that was a direct measure of the intended mRNA or protein target to investigate target engagement as part of the FIH clinical trial. Discussion Acknowledging the scarcity of available clinical data, this investigation sought to understand the dose selection process and types of FIH clinical studies conducted to support the development of oligonucleotide therapeutics from drug development between January 2015 and January 2024 and approved therapies between September 1998 and January 2024 ( Table 1 ). Specifically, we focused on considerations in dose selection, dose scaling approaches with considerations to the route of administration, FIH clinical trial design, target engagement, and pharmacodynamic biomarkers. We reviewed 89 distinct IND programs and 16 approved oligonucleotide therapeutics, commonly conducting SAD and/or MAD design clinical trials, to demonstrate that scaling approaches were often omitted from the FIH clinical trial protocol and when included, the majority of oligonucleotide therapeutics with systemic exposure utilized BSA adjustments for their scaling approach for FIH dose selection with a safety factor of at least 10. NOAELs from sensitive species were included in the majority of the FIH trial protocols (87.9%, n = 80) and were part of the dose selection justification ( Fig. 3 ). Finally, target engagement and pharmacodynamic endpoints were included in the majority of FIH clinical trials and transcripts or protein targets were the pharmacodynamic endpoint in 32.6% of the IND programs. Table 1. L ist of F ood and D rug A dministration -A pproved O ligonucleotide -B ased T herapies and T hier D osing S trategy Drug name Category Approval date Indication MOA Route of administration FIH dosing Approved dose Fomivirsen ASO 1998 Cytomegalovirus retinitis RNAse H1 IVT 330 μg, 75 μg in stage 1 and 150 μg in stage 2 for QW for 3 IVT injections and Q2W 300 μg Q4W Mipomersen ASO 2013 Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia RNAse H1 SC 3 weeks of SC dosing of 200 mg QW, 70 mg thrice weekly, and 30 mg QD 200 mg QW Eteplirsen ASO 2016 Duchenne muscular dystrophy PMO, exon skipping IV Study 33: 0.09 mg or 0.9 mg total IM; Study 28: 0.5 to 20 mg/kg IV Q7D for 12 weeks 30 mg/kg QW Nusinersen ASO 2016 Spinal muscular atrophy Exon inclusion IT 1 mg, 3 mg, 6 mg, and 9 mg IT 12.5 mg × 3 doses Q2W, 12.5 mg × 1 dose QM, and 12.5 mg Q4M Patisiran siRNA 2018 Hereditary ATTR RISC IV IV 0.01, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3, and 0.5 mg/kg 0.3 mg/kg Q3M Inotersen ASO 2018 Hereditary ATTR RNAse H1 SC SC 50 mg, 100 mg, 200 mg, 400 mg, and 300 mg 300 mg QW Golodirsen ASO 2019 Duchenne muscular dystrophy PMO, exon skipping IV 10 and 20 mg/kg QW and 30 mg/kg at week 7 30 mg/kg QW Givosiran siRNA 2019 Acute hepatic porphyria GalNAc-conjugated, RISC SC 2.5 and 5 mg/kg Q3M and 2.5 mg/kg and 5 mg/kg Q4W SC 2.5 mg/kg QM Viltolarsen ASO 2020 Duchenne muscular dystrophy PMO, exon skipping IV 1.25–20 mg/kg QW IV for 12 weeks 80 mg/kg QW Lumasiran siRNA 2020 Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 GalNAc-conjugated, RISC SC Part A: 0.3, 1, 3, or 6 mg/kg SC; Study 001 Part B: 1 mg/kg QM × 3 doses, 3 mg/kg QM × 3 doses, or 3 mg/kg Q3M × 2 doses 3 mg/kg or 6 mg/kg varied by BW and age Casimersen ASO 2021 Duchenne muscular dystrophy PMO, exon skipping IV 4, 10, 20, 30 mg/kg QW IV 30 mg/kg QW Inclisiran siRNA 2021 Primary hyperlipidemia, including heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia (HeFH) siRNA SC 25, 100, 300, 500, and 800 mg QM, Q2W, or QW In combination with statin therapy, 284 mg administered as a single subcutaneous injection initially, again at 3 months, and then every 6 months Vutrisiran siRNA 2022 Hereditary ATTR GalNAc-conjugated, RISC SC Single dose of 25 mg to 300 mg 25 mg total dose, Q3M Tofersen ASO 2023 ALS in adults who have a mutation in the SOD1 gene RNase H1 IT 10, 20, 40, 60, or 100 mg 100 mg per administration Eplontersen ASO 2023 Polyneuropathy of hereditary transthyretin-mediated amyloidosis in adults RNAse H1 SC 120 mg once or 45, 60, 90 mg Q4W for 12 weeks 45 mg once monthly Nedosiran siRNA 2023 Primary hyperoxaluria type 1 GalNAc-conjugated, RISC SC 0.3, 1.5, 3, 6, 12 mg/kg Age and body-weight tiered dosing, 160 mg QM for children (9–11 years of age) and adults and adolescents (12 years and older) who are more than or equal to 50 kg. For children (9–11 years of age) who are lesser than 50 kg, the recommended dosing regimen is 3.3 mg/kg QM. For adults and adolescents (12 years and older) who are lesser than 50 kg, the recommended dose is 128 mg QM) Open in a new tab ASO, antisense oligonucleotide; IT, intrathecal; IV, intravenous; SC, subcutaneous; QM, once monthly; Q3M, once every 3 months; Q4M, once every 4 months; QW, once weekly. FIG. 3. Open in a new tab Schematics of findings from investigational and approved oligonucleotide therapeutics. BSA, body surface area; BW, body weight; FIH, first-in-human; HNV, healthy normal volunteers; INDs, investigational new drugs; IC, cutaneous; ICV, intracerebroventricular; IHC, inhaled; IM, intramuscular; IT, intrathecal; IV, intravenous; IVT, intravitreal; MAD, multiple ascending dose; MTD, maximal tolerated dose; NOAEL, no observable adverse event level; RCT, randomized controlled trial; ROA, route of administration; SAD, single ascending dose; SC, subcutaneous. Oligonucleotide therapeutics have distinct molecular and pharmacokinetic properties compared with biologics and small molecules and may require different interspecies scaling and selection of dose considerations. Early phase trial design considerations need to account for relatively short pharmacokinetic half-life, long pharmacodynamic half-life, and extensive tissue distribution, impacting the clinical pharmacology assessment of these products. Nonclinical animal models with a sensitive species utilizing the intended clinical route of administration and conjugated form of the drug product, if applicable, should be leveraged to understand the safety profile of these products and inform early dose selection and dosing interval. Interspecies scaling has a wide range of exponents that use simple allometry, which differs based on type of species and the number of species, which has limited inference of physiological processes in exponents. Fixed exponents and scaling derived from a single species may be inappropriate, largely leading to erroneous predictions. However, clinical data are limited for the scaling approach most appropriate for oligonucleotide products, which achieves systemic concentrations. In this study, the majority of FIH clinical trials used NHPs, namely cynomolgus monkeys, to derive NOAEL for dose selection in the FIH clinical trial protocol. Overall, this analysis of programs observed NHPs as the most sensitive species across programs and may be most appropriate for scaling and dose selection. Healthy participants were enrolled in 34.8% of the investigational programs (31/89) and 37.5% of the approved therapeutics (6/16). The relatively low proportion of programs that included healthy volunteers may be due to novel chemistries, safety concerns with long pharmacodynamic half-life, class-based toxicities, and concerns related to the mechanism of action of the drug. For instance, many diseases treated with these oligonucleotide therapeutics are a result of gain-of-function mutations, and the impact of a knockout in healthy populations may have unknown impact (or diseases corresponding with reduced expression). Our results align with Rogers et al., which found that of 152 unique clinical studies, 20% of studies included healthy subjects, while the vast majority included patients. Subcutaneous, intrathecal, and intravenous were the most common routes of administration in our study and body weight-based dosing was utilized in over a third of clinical studies. 5 Target engagement and validation are relatively straightforward given the well-characterized target and are often correlated with target abundance. However, assays must be developed in early-stage development to guide dose selection and predict clinical efficacy. Nearly 75% of the investigational oligonucleotide therapeutics included target engagement or pharmacodynamic assays in their FIH clinical trial to improve the understanding of efficacy and dose selection. Specifically, a common target engagement assay is to quantify the change in RNA abundance depending on the background signal and if the basal concentration is below the lower limit of detection. Sometimes direct pharmacodynamic measurement may be difficult to measure in human (e.g., RNA expressed in hepatocytes) without requiring tissue biopsy. When it is not feasible to collect tissue biopsies, it is preferential to collect pharmacodynamic biomarkers that are proximal to the pathway of the targeted RNA. Challenges associated with membrane-bound protein quantification and low RNA synthesis may also lead to quantification under the limit of detection. However, these engagement assays are critically important to both the dose selection and determination of the dosing interval and require optimization. Challenges remain due to missing data and lack of granularity on BW versus BSA-based scaling decision-making in drug development. Simple allometric scaling to predict human plasma clearance using scaling data from one, two, or three animal species has shown mixed results for well-characterized oligonucleotide therapeutics such as 2-MOE ASOs. 7 However, scaling approaches based on BSA or BW were not included and only two out of the four ASOs evaluated met the acceptable prediction range. Other literature has specified that simple allometric scaling that utilizes multiple species is limited in value and may provide misleading results due to overutilization of mice, and thus, complex allometric scaling models that consider species organ weights/volumes, plasma protein binding, and other characteristics may provide better prediction. 8 Our data support that NHPs are the most sensitive species and BSA-based scaling provides a conservative starting dose in humans compared to BW-based scaling. Continued effort to compare sensitive species and scaling approaches is warranted to support use of rodent species as sensitive species. Overall, we provide the following considerations in the design of FIH clinical trials for oligonucleotide therapeutics based on the assessment of 89 investigational oligonucleotide drug products and 16 approved RNA-therapeutics: (1) generally, two species, including NHPs, for scaling may be useful to predict parameters for oligonucleotides expected to have systemic concentrations, (2) while no consensus was reached in our analysis, literature indicates BSA-based scaling may provide a conservative estimate in predicting human pharmacokinetic parameters; however, BW-based approaches are often utilized and more research is warranted to determine the optimal method, (3) design and validate a pharmacodynamic assay early in development to guide dose and dosing interval selection and proof of target engagement, and (4) where possible, leverage available information from other oligonucleotide therapeutics in the same chemical class. Acknowledgments We would like to thank Robert Schuck and Michael Pacanowski for providing thoughtful feedback for this article. Footnotes Author Disclosure Statement The authors do not have any disclosures. References 1. Agrawal N, Dasaradhi PV, Mohmmed A, et al. RNA interference: Biology, mechanism, and applications. Microbiol Mol Biol Rev 2003;67:657–685. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Sen GL, Blau HM. A brief history of RNAi: The silence of the genes. Faseb J 2006;20:1293–1299. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Crooke ST, Wang S, Vickers TA, et al. Cellular uptake and trafficking of antisense oligonucleotides. Nat Biotechnol 2017;35:230–237. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Scoles DR, Minikel EV, Pulst SM. Antisense oligonucleotides: A primer. Neurol Genet 2019;5:e323. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Rogers H, Adeniyi O, Ramamoorthy A, et al. Clinical pharmacology studies supporting oligonucleotide therapy development: An assessment of therapies approved and in development between 2012 and 2018. Clin Transl Sci 2021;14:468–475. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Note for Guidance on Toxicokinetics:. The Assessment Of Systemic Exposure In Toxicity Studies S3A. 1994. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Mahmood I. Pharmacokinetic allometric scaling of oligonucleotides. Nucleic Acid Ther 2011;21:315–321. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Yu RZ, Grundy JS, Henry SP, et al. Predictive dose-based estimation of systemic exposure multiples in mouse and monkey relative to human for antisense oligonucleotides with 2’-o-(2-methoxyethyl) modifications. Mol Ther Nucleic Acids 2015;4:e218. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Deng R, Iyer S, Theil FP, et al. Projecting human pharmacokinetics of therapeutic antibodies from nonclinical data: What have we learned? MAbs 2011;3:61–66. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Li C, Zhang C, Deng R, et al. Prediction of Human Pharmacokinetics of Antibody-Drug Conjugates From Nonclinical Data. Clin Transl Sci 2019;12:534–544. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Huh Y, Smith DE, Feng MR. Interspecies scaling and prediction of human clearance: Comparison of small- and macro-molecule drugs. Xenobiotica 2011;41:972–987. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Nanavati C, McMullen G, Yu R, et al. Interspecies Scaling of Human Clearance and Plasma Trough Exposure for Antisense Oligonucleotides: A Retrospective Analysis of GalNAc3-Conjugated and Unconjugated-Antisense Oligonucleotides. Nucleic Acid Ther 2021;31:298–308. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',448,'2025-10-15 09:35:23','2025-10-15 09:35:23');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4076,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9166288','"New Normal" Radiology.','MacDonald D, Reitzik S.','2022','extraoral panoply agp aaomr upholds gamut aaomr aaomr focusses alara ibws ebws dpr dpr dpr posteriorly proximally interproximal dpr endodontics ibw crowns supernumeraries ebws condyles ramus sextant fovs gaggers circa ghosting pocketing endodontics posteriorly proximally interproximal retreated endodontics imparted ibw dpr ibws fovs phosphor cmos ebw ibws dento alveolus retakes ibws fovs cmos semiconductor dpr ebw extraoral bitewing ibw bitewing sterilisation dpr forerunner bagging phosphor bagging minimised unclean gagger extraoral agp extraoral bitewings ebws extraoral extraoral bitewing posteriorly proximally interproximal extraoral endodontic radiolucencies ascend fryback thornbury forwards ascends fryback thornbury endodontically mesiobuccal radiolucency mesiobuccal mesiobuccal dpr dpr cavitated noncavitated magnify brightness dpr dpr cephalogram dprs radiolucencies nonvital dprs forwards atheromas dprs dpr dprs dprs ebw ebw posteriorly proximally ebw dpr coned sextant ebw dento crowns alveolus bitewings ibws ibws ebws ibws ebws superimpositions ebw crowns orthodontic phosphor photostimulable phosphors psps ccds semiconductor cmos brightness luminance brightness illuminance lux replaces chairside incipient bitewing devalue alara endeavours µsv bitewing phosphor dpr cbcts fovs dpr dpr dpr miniscule fovs endodontic fovs fovs mpr catenary dentoscan implantologists catenary implantologists endodontics mesiodistal obliquely mpr sextant backwards sextant mpr dentoscan mpr transaxial keratocyst keratocytocystic assuredly drifts sited colosi mupparapu shintaku p99 oooo cochin zibo facchin vaccines10010107 parums p167 p174 adaj samaranayake fakhreddin p972 p980 adaj alara alarp aa8e53 jarrom elston bmjebm caramello macciotta corrao carle waterfield photostimulable phosphor b93 dadlani chehroudi esteves aleksejuniene aae aaomr endodontic iej fryback thornbury 0272989x9101100203 rohlin lindh kaleidoscope dentomaxillofacial eht e2018017 farman p653 p654 nidcr baelum hintze danielsen nyvad eke borgnakke genco corbit s43 isd ghaeminia nienhuijs toedtling cd003879 pub5 jankowski bdj vertinsky farman scarfe atheroma p533 p547 oooo tyndall extraoral p444 p447 adaj noujeim langlais prihoda extraoral interproximal mfb takeshita bitewing stelt panoramische rontgenopnamen tandheelkundige diagnostiek tijdschr tandheelkd ntvt okshi rohlin robis wahid acm2 yoshimi','Abstract COVID-19, the most recent and globally impactful zoonotic viral pandemic in the last 20 years, has now entered its third year. As the global dental profession returns to providing as full a range of services as possible, in addition to embedding the new infection-control processes that were developed for this pandemic, it should also take full advantage of digital conventional radiology (intraoral, extraoral, and panoramic radiography) and cone-beam computed tomography. Regardless of vaccinations, new or yet-to-manifest variants, and testing, some dentists may be working in communities where the asymptomatic but potentially infectious patient poses a real risk. This needs to be met with not only the whole COVID-19 panoply the dentist is already too familiar with but also the need to minimise aerosol generation production by dental radiography. A flowchart and a table that compares the attributes of the above modalities are included. Keywords: COVID-19, Radiology, Radiography, Cone-beam computed tomography Introduction Radiology, particularly intraoral radiography, is central to the practice of dentistry. Unfortunately, it is an aerosol-generating procedure (AGP) and thus enhances the spread of infections, such as COVID-19. In January 2021, guidelines by the American Academy of Oral and Maxillofacial Radiology (AAOMR) 1 presented an appropriate dental radiologic response to COVID-19 just when worldwide vaccinations were beginning. Although “effectiveness of full vaccination is 88% against infection, 94% against hospitalisation, and 95% against death,” 2 the recent Omicron variant in the space of a few months has become the dominant strain in many countries. 3 Though less lethal than most of the earlier variants, its higher transmissibility and its high proportion of “breakthrough” amongst even the fully vaccinated suggest that vaccines alone will not end the pandemic. 4 Therefore, it is now important to take the next step and assist the dentist providing a fuller service to the community in what has become the “new normal” in this third year of the pandemic. This new normal is simply not just the provision of the full scope of dental treatment to patients who have been deprived of regular dental treatment by the pandemic for the last 2 years; it includes treatment for patients with “long COVID” now compromised by neurologic, cardiovascular, and clotting problems. 5 Although dentists did not experience the higher mortality of their medical colleagues, reflecting both the stringent infection control dentists applied globally and the fact that most patients with severe acute COVID-19 did not present to their dentists, 6 the present review upholds the full infection control gamut of the AAOMR''s guidelines, which are still required by most jurisdictions globally. Whilst the primary focus of AAOMR guidelines was on the dental emergency patient, 1 this review, summarised in Figure 1 and the Table , focusses on the fuller range of services provided by the general and/or specialist dentist to their regular patient and therefore the ever-present need to adhere to ALARA (as low as reasonably achievable). 7 This approach should be adapted by the public health directives and recommendations appropriate to the dentist''s jurisdiction. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Flowchart identifying the imaging modalities that may be available in dental offices and their application in patients presenting with a clinically indicated need for radiography and their COVID-19 status. CBCT, cone-beam computed tomography; FOV, field of view; PPE, personal protective equipment. This figure is a development of Figure 1 in MacDonald et al. 1 Table. A comparison of the attributes of 5 radiologic modalities now used in dentistry. Feature IBWs EBWs, Segmental DPR DPR CBCT Availability Most dental office Available in most modern DPR units Most dental offices Still fewer dental offices SR (fine detail) Best for caries Posteriorly focussed proximally optimised for interproximal surfaces of posterior teeth and therefore better than DPR for caries Moderate: Most units not good enough for caries, but adequate for review of the entire jaws and upper neck Least: But choose best SR (0.076-0.09 mm voxel size) for endodontics, impacted teeth, and small lesions Diagnostic efficiency Best for most studies of individual teeth Equivalent to IBW on basis of in vitro studies Lower half of sinus to upper neck Reveals entire jaws, from condyle to condyle and lower half of sinus to upper neck Best when cross-sectional information required Need for awareness of incidental findings Nothing beyond crowns and cervical half of roots Supernumeraries, lesions in lower sinus, posterior body of mandible and upper neck Same as EBWs and condyles, ramus and anterior sextant Particularly of base of skull and vertebrae in large FOVs Reduced patient compliance Children and gaggers and those prone to coughing Most current units require the patient to be standing or seated vertically; therefore, not ideal for elderly and sick patients Aerosol production likelihood Highest because of the above need to bag/barrier receptors Least, since nothing enters the oral cavity Positioning artefact likelihood High Low to moderate Low to moderate Low to moderate Movement artefact likelihood Minimal: short exposure time Moderate Moderate: circa 14-16 seconds High: up to 30 seconds Metal artefact likelihood None Provided patient properly prepared and positioned Provided patient properly prepared and positioned Greatest likelihood Ghosting artefact likelihood None Moderate Moderate to high Low Cross-sectional display None None None Best Optimal indications Caries and periodontal pocketing Single rooted endodontics Posteriorly focussed proximally optimised for interproximal surfaces of posterior teeth and thus better for caries Panoramic view of jaws, particularly in patients with multiple or complex pathology Complex or retreated endodontics and impacted teeth Radiation dose imparted Least Greater than IBW and lower than DPR Equivalent to 4 IBWs Highest even with small FOVs Other relevant comments Phosphor plates have a wider dynamic range than solid state (CCD and CMOS) receptors 1. 2 EBW images instead of 4 IBWs 2. Displays dento-alveolus of both jaws on one image 3. Reduced retakes compared to IBWs Anterior focal trough is narrower in many units and vertebral column is superimposed on it = artefacts Requires technical expertise and experience particularly with small FOVs Open in a new tab CBCT, cone-beam computed tomography; CCD, charge-couple device; CMOS, complementary metal-oxide semiconductor; DPR, dental panoramic radiograph; EBW, extraoral bitewing; FOV, field of view; IBW, intraoral bitewing; SR, spatial resolution. This table is a development of Table III in MacDonald et al. 1 Assessment of the patient with potential COVID-19 in 2022 Whilst it is clear that those patients with active COVID-19 or being investigated for it be treated only on an emergency basis, what about the likelihood of those who are infected but asymptomatic? There are 2 primary screening methods for COVID-19, questionnaires with temperature-taking and COVID-19 testing. A medical emergency room (ER) discovered that 60% of the patients who tested negative for COVID-19 had the infection, 8 thus indicating that false negatives through testing are an issue, even in a medical ER. As “more evidence is needed about the effectiveness of testing outside of hospital settings and in mild or asymptomatic cases,” 9 the dentist should continue to assume that each patient who is asymptomatic for COVID-19 might be infectious, as it is possible “that the oral cavity actively participates in SARS-CoV-2 transmission.” 10 Enhanced history-taking and donning and doffing personal protective equipment in the appropriate manner, effective room ventilation, and cleansing and sterilisation (or disinfection) of equipment 1 protect not only dentists and their patients but also particularly those with underlying conditions that place them at a heightened risk for a severe or fatal outcome if COVID-19 is contracted. 11 Changes in radiographic technology available to the dentist in this late COVID-19 pandemic stage The most important developments in dental radiology over the last 3 decades have been digital conventional radiography, which includes both intraoral and dental panoramic radiography (DPR) and cone-beam computed tomography (CBCT). An earlier encounter with severe acute respiratory syndrome (SARS) of 2003 and 2004, the forerunner of the SARS-CoV-2 virus of the current COVID-19 pandemic, raised concerns about digital intraoral radiography''s infection control, resulting in the development of an effective bagging/barrier system for the phosphor plate system. 12 Since then, effective bagging/barrier systems have been tested for solid-state receptors. 13 Unfortunately, these barriers do not prevent the generation and dispersal of aerosols into the clinical environment. Although it is possible to eliminate this with room cleaning with the appropriate Environmental Protection Agency (EPA)–equivalent agents and solutions, high-efficiency particulate air (HEPA) filters, or other exhaustion of air to the outside, as detailed elsewhere, 1 aerosol generation should be minimised if not avoided. Whilst most patients presenting for emergency dental treatment can be diagnosed by a clinical examination alone, some form of radiography may be required. This is particularly most likely when the patient is previously unknown to the dentist or has not presented regularly for routine care and/or for patients whose clinical presentation indicates that radiography is necessary to evaluate the problem more fully before appropriate treatment can be offered. Nevertheless, this avoidance of intraoral radiography presents the dentist with a conundrum. 14 Regardless of COVID-19 status, every prescription for radiography should be based on sound clinical indications arising from a proper history and clinic examination. 15 This dictated that radiography should be performed when clinically necessary. A strategy had been developed early on in the pandemic to minimise aerosol generation in an intraoral radiographic context, such as using a dual operator system with one “clean” operator, who handled only the computer and exposure operation, and the “unclean” operator, who handled only the patient and receptor. 1 This could not work when the patient was a child, a gagger, or someone who was resistant to intraoral radiography. Radiography in such cases should be restricted to extraoral modalities, which are intrinsically non-AGP. These are extraoral bitewings (EBWs) ( Figure 2 ) and extraoral periapical radiographs and CBCT ( Figure 1 and Table .). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Extraoral bitewing of a child. It, unlike the dental panoramic radiograph, is posteriorly focussed and proximally optimised for interproximal surfaces of posterior teeth. It is therefore better than dental panoramic radiography for identifying caries. In many cases, such reliance on extraoral technology may be the investigation of choice on the basis of best evidence. Small field-of-view (FOV) cross-sectional imaging of CBCT permits significantly better radiologic diagnosis and treatment planning of endodontic lesions, such as periapical radiolucencies ( Figure 3 ), missed canals, adjacent anatomy, and root fractures. 16 This study was one of the first and the largest so far to ascend to the mid-tiers of the 6-tier Fryback and Thornbury hierarchical model for efficacy, 17 these being diagnostic impact and treatment impact. Going forwards, it is hoped that more future research in dental radiology ascends even to Fryback and Thornbury''s highest tiers of patient outcome efficacy and societal efficacy. 18 Fig. 3. Open in a new tab a, A periapical radiographic display of a previously endodontically treated left maxillary molar. The recurrence of the infection is due to a second mesiobuccal root canal, which was previously missed. b, c, and d represent the 4-cm diameter field-of-view cone-beam computed tomography of this molar. The radiolucency encompasses the mesiobuccal root from apex to the alveolar crest. The cause is a missed second mesiobuccal canal as is clear in b, where only one canal has been filled. The relationship of the lesion with the adjacent anatomy, the floor of the maxillary sinus, is also displayed. Whilst the full-mouth survey (FMS) remained a routine element of the dental care for a century, it should not be prescribed routinely, but rather in response to a particular patient''s dental needs. This is now reinforced by the significant association between the FMS and salivary gland cancers. 19 Although the DPR, since its appearance in the 1960s, had developed to such a degree that DPR was considered by an authority a feasible alternative to the FMS, 20 others have questioned this advice with regards to caries. 20 Dental caries, although preventable and having experienced a general decline in prevalence, remains the most prevalent chronic disease in both children and adults, particularly where social disparities remain. 21 The 2 ultimate goals of radiography are being reproducible and accurate in the detection of caries and estimating their depth as well as distinguishing between cavitated and noncavitated lesions. 22 Nevertheless, recently, the visual-tactile approach was determined to be significantly more effective in the true-positive diagnosis of caries than radiography, which resulted in false positives. 23 In addition to caries, periodontal disease is a globally prevalent chronic disease. Although a recent study on the trends of this disease in America was unable to determine whether it was increasing or decreasing, the fact that people are living longer and are also retaining their teeth for longer means that it is “reasonable to speculate that there are increasing numbers of people and teeth with periodontitis.” 24 Although generally the FMS is considered optimal for assessment of periodontal bone loss, “the ability to zoom in and magnify defects and to adjust dynamically the contrast and brightness allows” the DPR “for vastly improved ‘visualization’ of bone levels and intra-bony defects.” 25 Regardless of the clinical indication, the entire image, particularly the DPR and lateral cephalogram, and CBCT data set should be reviewed for incidental findings (IFs) so that their treatment, if required, may be attended to in a timely manner. A review of the largest and most recent case series of digital DPRs amongst new patients found IFs in one-third of them. The most frequent IF was impacted teeth, particularly third molars. 26 Whether such asymptomatic impacted teeth are to be removed or retained varies with jurisdiction and will not be discussed further, except to mention a recent Cochrane Review, which reported “insufficient evidence” either for their routine removal or for their retention. 27 Other findings were areas of periapical sclerosis or radiolucencies, at 1.0% and 0.4%, respectively, which are suggestive of nonvital teeth requiring treatment. 26 One of the earliest of such studies revealed that although the majority of IFs were not registered in the clinical record, it is most likely that they were observed but were not considered for further investigation and treatment at that time. 28 Nevertheless, this raises 2 issues. In both reports, routine DPRs in new patients were required as standard practice. Going forwards, all radiographic investigations should be comprehensively reviewed to identify all IFs and to record them. Attention should also be paid to IFs such as calcified carotid artery atheromas on DPRs, as these may indicate the need for further assessment and treatment of cardiovascular disease. 29 Evolution in DPR technology introduced “sectional” DPRs 3 decades ago with the aim to restrict the area irradiated, thus reducing radiation dose. A species of these segmented DPRs is the EBW ( Figure 2 ). The EBW uses posteriorly focussed proximally optimised panoramic radiograph-like images with non-overlapping proximal surfaces of posterior teeth in a single scan. 30 Therefore, the EBW is different from a single DPR coned down to the posterior sextant. Although the EBW displays the entire dento-alveolar apparatus in contrast to only the crowns and adjacent roots and alveolus in intraoral bitewings (IBWs), it is primarily prescribed to assist in the diagnosis of caries, particularly in children and young adults. There was no significant difference in posterior proximal surface caries detection between IBWs and EBWs, except that detection of open proximal contact points was better for IBWs. 31 Such EBWs, based on clinical experience, may be rendered clinically completely useless due to the superimpositions of the secondary images of a heavily restored mouth. This is most likely to be observed on an EBW of an older adult patient, rather than in the child or younger adult. Nevertheless, stainless-steel crowns and ongoing fixed orthodontic treatment could similarly compromise its use. The gradual replacement of analogue (dental film) intraoral radiography by digital technologies—phosphor plate (also called photostimulable phosphors; PSPs) and solid state (charge-couple devices [CCDs] and complementary metal–oxide–semiconductor [CMOS])—since their development 3 decades ago, has been achieved to such a degree that a return to analogue/dental film is unlikely. Furthermore, these technologies create an environmentally safer workplace by replacing hazardous chemicals. A frequently overlooked aspect of digital radiography is the quality of image display (the inherent brightness and spatial resolution of the monitor), which is in turn dependent upon the viewing conditions. For analogue/film-based radiography, the viewing conditions include a bright light box with a luminance (brightness) of 1500 to 3000 cd/m 2 , as well as an illuminance (reduced ambient lighting) of 100 lux or less. In digital radiography, the display monitor, which replaces the light box, is less bright and indeed was not bright enough, which was a major problem 2 decades ago. Fortunately, in the intervening 2 decades the quality of monitors has so improved that most high-quality monitors are adequate for image display. 32 Although these should be viewed in a reduced ambient environment, rather than under the bright dental chairside light, 32 a selection of smartphones and tablets displayed statistically similar accuracy to detect incipient caries on digital bitewing radiographs. 33 Radiation dose The onset of COVID-19 does not in any way devalue our focus on ALARA, which endeavours to minimise the likelihood of radiation-induced lesions. These range from malignancies, such as oral and salivary cancers, benign brain tumours such as meningiomas, damage to the eyes (cataracts), and irradiation of the thyroid glands. The average radiation dose for one intraoral radiograph is less than 1.5 µSv based on an FMS consisting of 14 periapical radiographs and 4 bitewing radiographs, using phosphor plate or F-speed analogue/dental film. 34 Whilst the mean DPR was equivalent to 4 periapical radiographs, for some units this may be even fewer, whereas for others it may be equivalent to as many as 16 periapical radiographs. 34 Although the radiation dose of a CBCT can vary from about 8 periapical radiographs to more than 700, 34 according to a more recent report, the dosimetry of only 20% of the 279 units produced by 47 manufacturers have been included in systematic reviews (SRs). 35 Therefore, objective dosimetry on the majority of CBCTs is lacking. Furthermore, many SRs, though imprecise, noted that a reduced FOV height reduced the “effective dose” delivered to the patient and was best with small FOVs. 35 Almost every current CBCT has a DPR facility. Although an in vitro study revealed that CBCT-produced DPR images are similar to those produced on a stand-alone DPR unit, the latter''s advantage was that the spectrum of manually selected exposure factors for the child and adolescent patient prevented either over- or underestimation of the radiation dose. 36 Although radiation doses in dentistry overall are proportionally miniscule to those from medical sources, children are more susceptible to radiation-induced damage than mature adults and also have their whole lives ahead of them in which it may manifest. Therefore, minimise the radiation dose to children and young patients. CBCT: more on fields of view The size of FOV used should be that small enough to be confined to the jaws, as this is the traditional realm of the dentist. Medium and small FOVs are appropriate for implant, endodontic, impacted teeth, and lesion cases. Large FOVs include the eyes, the base of the skull, and the neck, 32 which are the proper realm of the medical doctor. If large FOVs are required for investigation of craniofacial anomalies, such as cleft lip and palate cases, the data sets should be reviewed by medical or oral and maxillofacial radiologists. Many dentists who review their own CBCT data sets only use the multiplanar reconstruction (MPR; Figure 4 A), which is the default reconstruction for most CBCT units. As this only produces axial, coronal, and sagittal reconstructions, which have been traditionally used in medical radiology, it is on its own generally inadequate to display the jaws, which follow a catenary arc. Before the advent of CBCT in clinical dentistry in 1999, the Dentoscan software was developed for medical computed tomography to assist implantologists by following the catenary arc selected for each patient. This is now available to CBCT as the “curved” or “panoramic” reconstructions ( Figure 4 B). Although it is most commonly used by implantologists, it contributes to endodontics ( Figure 2 ), where the 3-dimensional reconstruction of the tooth is displayed in its normal mesiodistal disposition rather than obliquely as by MPR. Both reconstructions can be used together when reviewing the data set of lesions ( Figure 4 ) and impacted teeth. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab A medium-sized field of view (cone-beam computed tomography [CBCT] of a neoplasm affecting the anterior sextant of the maxilla extending backwards into the posterior sextant and the anterior wall of the left maxillary sinus. (a) represents the multiplanar reconstruction (MPR), whereas (b) represents the “curved” or “panoramic” or formerly the Dentoscan reconstruction. Each reconstruction displays different aspects of the lesion. The MPR is the default reconstruction for most CBCT software, whereas the “curved” reconstruction is manually generated by first plotting out the alveolar arch on the axial reconstruction from which the panoramic and transaxial reconstruction are generated. The lesion is an odontogenic keratocyst, also known as a keratocytocystic odontogenic tumour. See MacDonald D. Oral and maxillofacial radiology: a diagnostic approach. 2nd Edn. British Columbia: Wiley-Blackwell. 2020. Conclusions Our response to COVID-19 has already prepared us for the next pandemic, which assuredly will arrive. This preparation may be achieved by “deep mutational scanning” observing “surface antigens drifts and shifts of the pathogens in silico using artificial intelligence and machine learning.” 6 In the meanwhile, the dentists should review all existing technology, particularly radiographic, adapting and developing it further so that it not only addresses new infection control challenges, reducing the radiation dose to their patients, but also improves the quality of the display of the patient and the accompanying disease. This also means developing technologies that do not use ionising radiation, such as qualitative light-induced fluorescence for the diagnosis of early caries 37 and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) optimally used for temporomandibular joint diseases 38 and lesions of the salivary glands and other soft-tissue of the face, jaws, and upper neck. Although MRI units are generally only sited in medical facilities, ultrasonography can be used in the dental office. Conflict of interest None disclosed. Funding This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. REFERENCES 1. MacDonald DS, Colosi DC, Mupparapu M, Kumar V, Shintaku WH, Ahmad M. Guidelines for oral and maxillofacial imaging: COVID-19 considerations. Oral Surg Oral Med Oral Pathol Oral Radiol. 2021;131(1):P99–110. doi: 10.1016/j.oooo.2020.10.017. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Cochin S, Zibo F, Facchin G, et al. The effectiveness of a diverse COVID-19 vaccine portfolio and its impact on the persistence of positivity and length of hospital stays: the Veneto region''s experience. Vaccines (Basel) 2022;10(1):107. doi: 10.3390/vaccines10010107. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Parums DV. Editorial: the 2022 World Health Organization (WHO) priority recommendations and response to the Omicron variant (B.1.1.529) of SARS-CoV-2. Med Sic Monit. 2022;28 doi: 10.12659/MSM.936199. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Adam D. Will Omicron end the pandemic? Here''s what experts say. Nature. 2022;602:20–21. doi: 10.1038/d41586-022-00210-7. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. France K, Glick M. Long COVID and oral health c