INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4116,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4824185','Conducting Rapid Street Assessment of Drug Users in New York City Using Oral Fluid and Brief Interviews: A Feasibility Study.','Aikins R, Hoefinger H, Guarino H, Rosenblum A, Magura S, Joseph H.','2015','suboxone oxycontin ecstasy molly adderall ritalin spice cathinones intrusive bathroom unfeasible intrusive tampering triangulate soup kitchens vans dsas oasas conceal streets congregating frequented shelters unstably remunerated locales frequented shelters corners frequented flatbush bedford stuyvesant brooklyn ridgewood queens downtown uptown harlem sidewalks benches walkway handrails overheard rbs halfway hashish hallucinogens ecstasy spice hydromorphone tapentadol benzodiazapines ritalin adderall depressants chewed swallowed swallowed sublingually sniffed snorted dealer forged stole halfway bendiner schleisinger alere bendiner schlesinger alere bendiner schlesinger suboxone alere monoacetylmorphine bendiner schlesinger alere quantisal wand peel wrapper wand cheek quantisal wands preservative refrigeration residentially shelters mepedrine hydromorphone hallucinogen synthetics hydromorphone opana tapentadol insufflating snorting cookers cottons rinse attire demeanor ethnographers minimalist attire happily patiently polite banter passersby approachability humanization queue queuing disappointed ineligibility ineligibility disappointment inconspicuous intrigue onlookers acquaintances weapon momentarily inconveniences traceable assurances venders bendiner schlesinger alere quantisal mouths mistook hotlines boroughs remarking polydrug corroborates polydrug catapres molly dimethoxy iodophenethylamine 25i nbome psychedelics mushrooms detectability euphoria lucid cogent amend malingering misreporting oxys uppers ingesting locales boroughs diversify expediently impatient saturate receipts mailers magura replicable unprescribed diverted nonn depen depen uhlmann debeck simo montaner metham phetamine bowersox arruda bourgois downtown bruneau arruda jutras aswad lankenau teti harocopos treese s11524 magura casriel magura magura spybrook rosenblum villano betzler undiag nosed crouch rollins fatah gorodischer burtin bosker acb tsemberis stefancic wacey golub arrestees magura nwakeze rosenblum soup fendrich wislar hubbell spiehler nio magura nuttbrock rosenblum magura mcquistion brodening golub dunlap wallman golub dunlap misdemeanor criminol rosenblum parrino schnoll magura haddox depen inciardi surratt cicero ultrarapid j069v11n01_08 shoveller healthplace rachlis montaner healthplace khoshnood stopka santelices criminalization beh bachman schulenberg nsduh usdohahssaamhsacfbhsaq watters chitwood altrix altrix birkenhead aleretoxicology certus seale dunlap ethnogr tb03911 valdimarsdottir jandorf redd abuses s0738 tabbarah nowalk raymund jewell suther kiros 0b013e3181ab22aa marsch guarino aponte melendez grabinski jsat magura laudet goldberger mieczkowski neale depen s0376 depen yacoubian arrestee gronholm lillsunde s0379 bosker theunissen conen sobriety s00213 watters biernacki stueve sandoval blome magura evalprogplan','Abstract This study piloted the feasibility of rapidly collecting both self-reports of drug use and saliva specimens for drug toxicology in field settings. The use of oral fluid collection devices to supplement self-reports is unproven in street settings and may pose challenges for field research. Sixty adults who identified as recent illicit drug users were recruited in public settings in New York City and were asked to complete a brief drug screening inventory and provided saliva specimens. Descriptive findings are detailed along with critical best research practices and limitations that provide important directions for researchers looking to employ both toxicology and self-report in rapid field recruitment designs. Keywords: Drug abuse screening, oral fluid, saliva, toxicology, street drug users, rapid assessment Introduction Although there are previous studies of illicit drug use by street-involved individuals, 1 – 4 there is little systematic research with this population that includes newer drugs and non-prescribed medications which are subject to abuse, including buprenorphine (Suboxone R ), methamphetamine (“Speed,” “Meth,” “Crystal”), prescription opioids (e.g., oxycodone [OxyContin R ]), methamphetamine/MDMA (“Ecstasy” “Molly”), prescription stimulants (e.g., Adderall R , Ritalin R ), synthetic marijuana (“K2,” “Spice”), and synthetic cathinones (“Bath Salts”). In addition, previous research with this population has relied exclusively on self-reporting of both traditional illicit drugs (e.g., heroin, cocaine, marijuana) and non-prescribed medications, 5 – 7 even though substantial under-reporting of illicit drug use in high-risk populations is common and long known, even under conditions of research confidentiality 8 – 10 Urinalysis is an established method of verifying self-reports of drug use, but is not practical for individuals recruited “on the street.” The collection of urine specimens is intrusive and requires access to a private bathroom, making it unfeasible, or at least inconvenient, for most street data collection. The collection of saliva specimens, in contrast, can be readily accomplished in public settings. The collection process is relatively non-intrusive, the specimen collection and storage devices are small and portable, and no special facilities are needed. Additionally, researchers can directly observe the collection of saliva in the field in order to minimize tampering or other variables that may affect the validity of results. 11 , 12 There has been considerable work in developing improved saliva tests for drugs subject to abuse. However, the accuracy of saliva testing varies greatly by the type of substance, specimen collection device and testing method; this is an active area of research and development. Saliva analysis also has a shorter window of detection than urinalysis for most drugs, which among other limitations, makes it unclear whether saliva would be as sensitive as urine for detecting use in a population of active street-involved drug users. 13 , 14 Epidemiological studies of illicit substance use among street-involved populations are important for monitoring trends in the prevalence of illicit substance abuse in communities and especially for detecting emerging substances of abuse. Street-involved populations are susceptible to high rates of illicit substance use, homelessness, and co-occurring mental illness. 15 Thus, conducting field research in public settings is an effective way to find and recruit this “at-risk” population. Furthermore, focusing on active drug users within such groups represents an important public health domain because it can detect emerging drug use trends (e.g., misuse of recently marketed prescription opioids) and associated risk behaviors. Typical survey approaches based on self-reports of illicit drug use almost certainly result in underestimates of use. 9 , 16 – 18 Previous studies that triangulate self-report and toxicology data have been conducted in controlled settings, including police booking facilities, 19 drug treatment clinics, 8 , 20 soup kitchens, 17 and even mobile medical vans, 21 but never in public settings. Saliva testing for street-involved populations could be useful, but there are no data on the feasibility or accuracy of this procedure for this population. New York City (NYC) represents an environmental extreme in that it is the most densely populated urban area in the United States and historically has the largest number of heroin users, 2 but also high numbers of cocaine, 22 marijuana, 23 and, more recently, prescription opioid users. 2 , 24 , 25 A NYC Street Research Unit was operated by the New York State Division of Substance Abuse Services (DSAS; later renamed as the Office of Alcoholism and Substance Abuse Services, or OASAS) for over 20 years and served a “vital and indispensable part of the research capacity of the agency” 26 This unit relied on ethnographic techniques to investigate street drug markets and drug use trends in NYC and to also collect a variety of artifacts used to prepare, administer, and conceal drugs, from which researchers concluded that “there is no better way to gather current information on drug activity on the streets of NYC.” 26 In the spirit of this early important ethnographic drug research with street-involved populations, and in order to pilot the feasibility of saliva testing for street-involved populations, the aims of the current study are to determine the feasibility of obtaining saliva specimens for drug testing from street-involved individuals congregating in venues known to be frequented by illicit drug users; to determine the sensitivity of a typical, commercially available saliva screening test for detecting the admitted use of illicit drugs or non-prescribed medications in street-involved populations; and to determine the potential utility of saliva screening to help estimate prevalence rates of illicit drug or non-prescribed medication use in street-involved populations. Field research with street-involved populations of drug users 27 , 28 often carries its own challenges and ethical dilemmas, including issues related to obtaining informed consent, maintaining confidentiality, safety, and risks of participant or research staff arrest. 29 Accordingly, an additional aim of this article is to discuss many of the salient challenges encountered by the research team in the field in order to help advance the development of practical guidelines for researchers looking to employ both toxicology and self-report in rapid field recruitment designs. Methods Overview of Procedures Between April and December of 2013, a convenience sample of 60 participants in NYC was recruited from accessible outdoor locations where drug users were likely to be found. For the purposes of this study, “street-involved” individuals were defined as those who could be readily observed “hanging out” in public locations (e.g., parks, homeless shelters, outside drug treatment facilities, etc.). This recruitment method was intended to yield a high proportion of homeless/unstably housed individuals and drug treatment patients, among others who were likely to meet eligibility criteria. Eligible participants had to be at least 18 years of age, speak English, and self-report use of at least one illegal drug or non-medical use of at least one psychoactive prescription medication within the past 30 days. Drug use “within the past 30 days” is a common survey and measurement standard in national health and other drug use research studies to represent recent substance use. 30 , 31 After staff confirmed eligibility and participants provided verbal informed consent, members of the research team conducted a brief structured interview and collected participants'' saliva in sterile devices. The data collection procedures were designed to be completed in 10–15 minutes and participants were remunerated US$15. Saliva specimens were mailed to the laboratory within 24 hours of collection. All procedures were approved by the Institutional Review Boards (IRBs) of National Development and Research Institutes and Western Michigan University. Due to the condition of participant anonymity, the provision of written informed consent was waived and only verbal consent was required. The data were analyzed with SPSS v. 20.0 (SPSS, Chicago, IL). Research Team Members of the research team had all been previously trained in human subject research and received additional training by the project''s principal investigator to familiarize them with safe recruitment and remuneration procedures, the administration and handling of saliva collection devices, and the safe accessing of locales frequented by the target population. Finally, researchers were given a list of drug classes and prescription drug brand names for both their own and participants'' reference, as well as a list of local shelters, drug treatment programs, and social service organizations to provide to participants upon request or as needed. Recruitment and Data Collection Sites Recruitment took place in parks and street corners that were known to be frequented by street-involved drug users, in areas including Flatbush; Bedford-Stuyvesant; East New York; Brooklyn; Ridgewood; Queens; downtown, midtown, and uptown (Harlem); Manhattan; and the South Bronx. Researchers were instructed to recruit participants from a diverse range of locations to avoid oversampling from any single location or type of venue. The average number of interviews conducted per day at any single site was three. Researchers entered the field alone, and interviews were mostly conducted during daylight hours for logistical and safety reasons. Recruitment and interviewing typically took place in parks, on sidewalks, and other public spaces. Interviews were usually conducted in seated locations in these venues (e.g., park benches, walkway handrails, or a private room at a harm reduction organization). Researchers always took the participants “aside” so that the interviews would not be overheard. Saliva collection was often visible, however. Upon approaching potential participants or upon being approached, researchers identified themselves, briefly outlined the goals and procedures of the study and screened interested individuals. If eligible, participants were asked to provide verbal consent and were informed about the participation incentive. Participants were asked to not provide any identifying information, and they were informed of their right to decline to answer any question without penalty. Interview Protocol The interview protocol consisted of 30 main questions which elicited information on: demographic characteristics (e.g., age, gender, race, ethnicity, etc.); substance use within the past 30 days; routes of drug administration; and injection-related risk behavior. Questions related to injection risk behavior were adapted from the Risk Behavior Survey 32 (RBS); all other items were constructed by the investigators. About halfway through the study it was observed that the saliva test''s sensitivity to detecting admitted drug use was low for some drugs; thus questions were added asking about substance use in the past three days, to better accord with the detection window for saliva screening. Although firm data are difficult to come by, specialists in saliva drug screening believe most drugs remain detectable for up to 2–3 days after administration. 33 Specific drugs and drug classes queried about were: marijuana/hashish, heroin, cocaine/crack, phencyclidine (PCP), methamphetamine, hallucinogens, Ecstasy (MDMA), “synthetic” marijuana (K2, Spice), bath salts, prescription opioids (buprenorphine, codeine, fentanyl, hydrocodone, hydromorphone, meperidine, methadone, morphine, oxycodone, tapentadol, tramadol, and “other” prescription opioids), non-opioid prescription drugs (benzodiazapines, stimulants [e.g., Ritalin, Adderall], anti-depressants, muscle relaxants, and sleep medicines). The instrument distinguished between medical and non-medical prescription drug use. The interview also asked participants to identify their first and second most frequently used drugs including routes of administration (e.g., injected, chewed and swallowed, swallowed without chewing, dissolved sublingually sniffed/snorted, smoked, etc.), reasons for use (e.g., to get high, to control withdrawal, to manage physical pain, to help sleep, etc.), and means by which they obtained the drug (dealer, friend or relative, forged prescription, stole it, internet, etc.). The final section of the protocol contained items about injection drug-related behaviors that can transmit HIV and hepatitis C and substance use treatment history, if applicable. Oral Fluid (OF) Collection and Laboratory Protocol Two laboratories were used for the present study, although not by original research design. Approximately halfway through planned recruitment, the original laboratory contracted for this study to screen and provide results of saliva toxicology, Bendiner & Schleisinger Medical Laboratories was sold and arrangements could not be made with the acquiring company to continue the study. Data collection was temporarily suspended until a new laboratory (Alere Toxicology) was obtained. A total of 35 samples were processed by Bendiner & Schlesinger and 25 by Alere. Bendiner & Schlesinger screened for methadone, opiates (non-specific), fentanyl, buprenorphine (Suboxone R ), oxycodone, cocaine, benzodiazepines, methamphetamine, marijuana (THC), and PCP. Alere Labs screened for all but two of the same drugs (oxycodone and fentanyl were excluded), with the addition of a unique metabolite of heroin (6-monoacetylmorphine [6-MAM]). The change of laboratories coincided with the aforementioned addition of past 3-day drug use questions to the interview protocol. The two laboratories used different OF collection devices. Bendiner & Schlesinger supplied the Intercept ® Oral Specimen Collection Device; Alere Toxicology supplied the Quantisal ™ Saliva Collection Device. Enzyme immunoassay was the screening technique in both cases, with presumptive positive results confirmed by gas chromatography-mass spectrometry (GC/MS) or liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (LC/MS) methodology. The sealed collection wand was given to participants, who were instructed to peel the outer wrapper off the wand and place the cotton swab end against the inside of their cheek. Saliva collection with Intercept took place after the interview. Since Quantisal required a longer collection period (at least 5 minutes), the procedure was amended to begin saliva collection prior to the interview. In either case participants knew before the interview that saliva would be requested. For both devices, following OF collection, participants inserted the wands swab-side-down into plastic tubes containing the preservative liquid. The devices were then sealed and labeled by the researcher and the specimens were express-mailed to the laboratory within 24-hours of collection. The specimens did not require refrigeration. Results Characteristics of Respondents The sample consists of 60 participants (15 women, 45 men), ranging from 19 to 67 years old with a mean age of 47 years ( SD = 12); 62% identified as African American, 27% as Hispanic or Latino, and 13% as Caucasian. The majority of the sample were residentially unstable (55% were either homeless or stayed in shelters); 35% were currently in substance abuse treatment (e.g., methadone maintenance, residential treatment, outpatient treatment), and an additional 35% stated that they had been in substance use treatment in the past. Self-Reported Drug Use Substance use reported within the sample is shown in Table 1 and should be interpreted cautiously in the context of the eligibility criteria (street-involved populations who were pre-screened for past month illicit drug or non-medical prescription drug use) and the relatively small sample size. Marijuana and prescription opioids were the most frequently reported drugs used within the past 30 days, followed by benzodiazepines. The most frequently self-reported opioids used within the past 30 days were methadone, a oxycodone, codeine hydrocodone, buprenorphine, and morphine. Past month use of tramadol, mepedrine, and hydromorphone were each reported once. Table 1. Self-Reported Past 30-Day Use. Self-Reported “Yes” Past 30-Day Use Substance n (60) % Marijuana 33 55.0 Methadone 25 41.7 Benzodiazepine 25 41.7 Cocaine (not crack) 24 40.0 Heroin 21 35.0 Crack cocaine 20 33.3 Oxycodone 20 33.3 Codeine 13 21.7 Hydrocodone 12 20.0 Hallucinogen/MDMA 5 8.3 Phencyclidine 4 6.7 Buprenorphine 4 6.7 Morphine 4 6.7 Synthetics 3 5.0 Stimulant medications 3 5.0 Methamphetamine 1 1.7 Hydromorphone 1 1.7 Meperidine 1 1.7 Tramadol 1 1.7 Opana 1 1.7 Fentanyl 0 0.0 Tapentadol 0 0.0 Open in a new tab Routes of drug administration were recorded for the substances that participants identified as either their primary or secondary drugs of choice. The majority of primary or secondary heroin users reported intranasal use (13/17 = 76.5%) as opposed to injection use of heroin (4/17 = 23.5%). Participants also reported insufflating (e.g., “snorting”) prescription drugs, including buprenorphine, oxycodone, and benzodiazepines. Participants who reported any lifetime injection drug use were asked about injection risk behaviors (e.g., sharing needles, cookers, cottons, rinse water, water containers, or splitting the drug solution using front-loading or back-loading), and one of the four injection drug users reported engaging in each of these behaviors within the past month. Saliva Test Results Toxicologies in comparison with self-reported past 30- and 3-day use are shown in Tables 2 and 3 , respectively. The sensitivity of the saliva test is defined as the percentage of tests that are positive when the individual reports using the drug. Sensitivities reported in Table 2 were not expected to be high because the window of detection for OF is only up to three days for drugs, whereas the main analysis is based on past 30 day reports. Nevertheless, saliva-positive results should be more likely when the individual reports drug use than when the individual does not, which would be reflected in a statistically significant association. Table 2. Self-Reported Past 30-Day Use Versus Saliva Toxicology. Self-Reported Past 30 Day Use No (%) Yes (%) Total ( n ) Saliva test—cocaine/crack Negative 24 (96) 15 (45) 39 Positive 1 (4) 18 (55) 19 Total 25 (100) 33 (100) 58 (Fisher''s exact test = .00; sensitivity = 55%) Saliva test—opiates Negative 28 (100) 22 (73) 50 Positive 0 (0) 8 (27) 8 Total 28 (100) 30 (100) 58 (Fisher''s exact test = .01; sensitivity = 27%) Saliva test—methadone Negative 33 (97) 6 (25) 39 Positive 1 (3) 18 (75) 19 Total 34 (100)