ller sample size. Associations with marijuana and benzodiazepines were not significant. None of the 11 self-reports of oxycodone were detected by saliva. The range of sensitivities for different drugs was large, between 0% (oxycodone) and 75% (methadone; Table 2 ). Limiting self-reports to the past three days increased the sensitivities of saliva testing for cocaine/crack, methadone, marijuana, and benzodiazepines, but not opiates generally or heroin specifically. The range of sensitivities for different drugs was again large, between 25% (opiates, heroin, and benzodiazepines) and 100% (methadone; Table 3 ). Not shown in Tables 2 and 3 are positive tests/3- and 30-day self-reports for methamphetamine (0/1), PCP (1/4), and buprenorphine (0/4). There were very few instances where a respondent denied the use of a drug or medication, but the saliva test was positive. Challenges and Best Practices in Fieldwork Respondents were typically recruited in sets of three to six in a single recruitment episode, and were generally quite willing to participate— remuneration was helpful and necessary. “Impression management” 34 was important in gaining trust and building rapport, which entailed intentional choices by the research staff regarding attire, demeanor, and speaking characteristics in the field. 34 , 35 Self-presentation bias can have a significant effect on the quality of interactions between research ethnographers and drug using populations, 36 and field researchers found the self-presentation strategies employed to be effective. These strategic choices included wearing minimalist casual clothing (as opposed to more formal attire found in academic or clinical settings), and investing considerable time happily and patiently observing a setting before deliberately making polite eye contact and engaging in conversational banter with as many passersby as possible. The overall impression to bring into field settings should ultimately be one of approachability and humanization for both the participant and process. Conducting recruitment only during the daytime and in public areas was another intentional consideration to maintain safety in the field both for participants and the research team. Recruiting from small networks was often helpful whereby the recruitment of a single participant often led to more. However, this sometimes led to a small queue of people waiting to participate, at which point the maintenance of privacy for those being interviewed became a concern that required active management by the research interviewer. The queuing and monetary incentive sometimes resulted in disappointed individuals who could not be enrolled due to ineligibility, a lack of OF kits, or saturation from a single recruitment site. Some individuals who were approached refused participation and several prospective participants were turned away due to ineligibility (i.e., no illicit drug use within past 30 days) or due to researchers either running out of OF kits or limiting the number of participants who could be recruited at a single site. The setting of expectations was crucial to prevent disappointment from escalating to conflict. Because recruitment via peer referral frequently took place, researchers often found it important to disclose to enrolled participants the number of kits in their possession to convey a clear understanding of exactly how many interviews could be completed. The OF devices and interview process were generally inconspicuous, but field researchers found that the brief procedure could provoke some curiosity in populated public places. Such intrigue came not only from onlookers and participants'' acquaintances, but law enforcement officials as well. One interview, for example, was interrupted when police officers had to remove a participant from a public park for consuming alcohol and possessing a weapon (a hammer). Thus, the interview had to be momentarily postponed and continued outside of park grounds. Rare logistical inconveniences aside, the occasional presence of law enforcement or staff of local social service organizations did not interfere with study procedures. Participants were generally cooperative in providing the saliva specimens and the devices were relatively simple for both the researchers and participants to use without error or confusion in handling. In one instance a participant refused to give a specimen, for fear that her DNA would be traceable despite the researcher''s assurances of anonymity and the fact that no identifying information was collected. Prior research has shown that a fear of genetic testing and mistrust of the medical establishment in general is especially pervasive among African Americans, Latin Americans, and groups of lower socioeconomic standing, 37 – 39 thus perhaps accounting for this type of occasional reluctance from participants. The transition of laboratory venders provided added insight into the use of two different commercial devices. During the first half of the study, Bendiner & Schlesinger''s Intercept OF collection devices were administered after completion of interview/survey, requiring 1–2 minutes to become sufficiently saturated. Alere''s Quantisal OF devices, however, had a blue chemical indicator that took up to 5 minutes to signal that a sample had been collected. Therefore, to expedite the process and minimize inconvenience to participants, researchers found it necessary to begin OF collection at the beginning of the interview. This meant that participants completed the interview with OF collection devices in their mouths, which, fortunately did not impede speech or provide any difficulty. Testing these devices among all members of the research team during training was an important step before beginning fieldwork and should be viewed as essential for similar research designs, no matter which devices are used. Early in the study, some researchers found that participants requested information about housing or substance abuse treatment or mistook researchers for social workers or outreach workers. Therefore, the team began offering a two-page resource information sheet to the participants after they completed all research procedures. This resource list contained information about local syringe exchanges/harm reduction sites, substance abuse and mental health services, HIV/AIDS support, and crisis hotlines in all five NYC boroughs. Participants who requested or accepted this information were overwhelmingly grateful to receive it, with many remarking that it was of equal or greater value than the remuneration. Discussion and Limitations Some interesting substantive findings emerged from this pilot study with regard to polydrug use, new designer drugs, routes of administration, and HIV risk behaviors. One such finding is that about three times as many primary or secondary heroin users reported intranasal as opposed to injection use, which corroborates patterns and trends typically seen in research samples of heroin users. 40 While the prevalence of heroin insufflation compared with injection has not been assessed for broad heroin-using populations nationally, longitudinal data collected from NYC treatment programs between 1989 and 1999 noted that injection use declined from 71% to 39%, while insufflation increased from 25% to 59% over that decade-long period. 40 Similarly, a rapid survey of NYC street drug users 2 (mean age = 42 years) found equal prevalences of intranasal and injection heroin use (22% each), and a recent study of methadone maintenance patients (primarily heroin users, mean age = 41 years) found that 51% reported injection as their primary route of administration while 45% reported insufflation as primary. 2 , 41 With regard to polydrug use, a methadone maintenance patient reported using blood pressure medication (clonidine/Catapres R ) in combination with benzodiazepines to create a stronger high. Although older respondents were more likely to report cocaine/crack, heroin, and methadone use, younger respondents more often reported “designer” synthetic drugs such as “Molly” (MDMA), “2C-I” (2,5-dimethoxy-4-iodophenethylamine), “25-I” (25I-NBOMe, a derivative of 2C-I), as well as “K2” (synthetic marijuana), and more traditional psychedelics such as mushrooms and LSD. Future designs should pay attention to the association of age with certain types of drug use across larger samples. Cocaine/crack and methadone were detected by saliva with the highest sensitivities. This is consistent with previous research. 42 – 45 In fact, using GC/MS, self-reported cocaine use up to 4 days ago can be very reliably detected with sensitivity = 91%. 42 It should be noted that in the current study, almost all of the methadone use reported was by patients in methadone treatment, where daily doses are administered. Previous studies have reported the low sensitivity of saliva screening tests for benzodiazepines 44 , 46 and for marijuana 43 , 46 , 47 in field settings. This was reflected in the findings of the current study. On the other hand, previous research reports good detectability of opiates in saliva screening tests, 43 – 46 whereas the current study found low sensitivity to opiates generally and heroin specifically, whether compared with 30-day or 3-day self-reports. This may be attributable to the amount of opiates used; e.g., many respondents might be using just enough to avoid withdrawal symptoms, but not enough to achieve euphoria. To better understand the different sensitivities of saliva screening for different drugs, more detailed information on dosages and frequencies of use would need to be collected. The relatively low sensitivities of the saliva test for several drugs may also be influenced by limitations of self-reported drug use data. Participants were asked to report both 30-day (and later, past-3 day) drug use, which could be difficult for many to do accurately, especially in a population asked to recall psychoactive substances that can impair memory. Participants had to be lucid and cogent in order to enroll and were turned away if functionally impaired. Several participants, however, mentioned during interviews that they were under the influence of one or more substances. In some cases, respondents remembered drugs they had used and researchers had to go back and amend their answers. As with any study involving a remuneration incentive, there may have been some instances of over-reporting. And given the vulnerability of this population, under-reporting is also a possibility due to legal or stigma-related concerns. The extent of laboratory or specimen handling error is also unknown, and one sample was lost in the laboratory transition. To mitigate malingering or misreporting, researchers often went back over certain questions to double-check that they had all the information correct. The survey instrument provided multiple opportunities to report prescription drug use, but the various brand and chemical names for prescription drugs could be particularly confusing for respondents. The use of a reference sheet with prescription drug brands and chemical names was certainly helpful, but reporting accuracy could be further improved by including images of pills to improve recall. Another reason for the lack of concordance between self-reports and toxicologies may derive from respondents'' lack of specific drug knowledge, particularly relating to opioid formulations. In many cases, respondents were often not told explicitly what they were purchasing or taking, nor were they concerned about that at the time of acquisition or use. For example, several reported using “oxys” or “uppers” without any more detailed information about dosage, brand, or release formulation. One respondent, for example, reported taking “any other pill that was available” when heroin was unavailable. There was no evidence that oxycodone can be detected by a saliva screening test in a field setting, which is a limitation since this is currently a major drug of abuse. However, this result could have been affected by respondents'' possible inability to accurately distinguish the numerous opioids they were ingesting, as discussed. Finally, as this is a feasibility pilot study, the generalizability of findings are limited by non-probability sampling and the relatively small sample size. Because researchers relied on both convenience sampling in common drug-using locales and peer recruitment, there was the potential for participant selection bias. Recruiting participants from multiple sites in all NYC boroughs was helpful to diversify the sample, but recruitment could be made more systematic in future studies through the use of targeted sampling and ethnographic mapping of locations 48 or time-space sampling. 49 Conclusions and Implications for Practice This pilot study suggests that the rapid dual collection of self-report and OF data in the field is logistically feasible. However, it is hard to imagine this target population participating in similar research without remuneration, the condition of anonymity and a waiver of written informed consent. In addition to not collecting identifiable information, researchers should pay attention to impression management to more expediently develop trust and rapport. Administering OF collection devices at the beginning of the interview was helpful. Respondents could become impatient waiting for the swabs to saturate after the interview was over. Street-involved individuals should be approached with care when in groups and for the sake of researcher safety, it is very important that expectations are clearly set when offering remuneration. Organization is especially important in street settings and it is strongly recommended that all of the various paperwork (interview protocols, lab cover sheets, drug information cards, resource sheets, cash incentives, and incentive receipts) and OF devices (including hygienic envelopes for the sealed biological samples and postal mailers for the labs) be pre-sorted into individual field kits to expedite the process of moving between respondents in the field. In particular, field researchers should place cash or other remuneration incentives in envelopes to avoid showing cash in street settings. Any labeling or processing that can be properly done post-recruitment (e.g., completing lab forms and preparing collected samples to be mailed) should be done outside of the field in order to maximize peer recruitment opportunities at any given site. Drug information/reference cards containing images of actual prescription drugs may help respondents'' recall and aid the accuracy of self-report data. Furthermore, resource information sheets containing local shelter, social service, and substance abuse treatment locations should be mandatory for researchers to provide to respondents upon request. Provision of referral information and resource sheets need not be limited to respondents, because individuals who are either ineligible or decline to participate may still benefit from receiving such information upon request. These procedures help contribute to the beneficence of field research with street-involved substance users. The low to moderate sensitivities of saliva screening for detecting the past 30-day or even the past 3-day drug use does not mean that the value of saliva testing is questionable. On the contrary, such testing can be employed to determine true prevalences of drug use by applying the back calculation technique recently described by Magura (2010). 50 For instance, 55% of those who admit cocaine use in the past 30 days tested positive in the current study. From this one could infer that, among a hypothetical set of individuals who deny cocaine use, but 10% of whom test positive, the true prevalence of cocaine use can be estimated as 10% × (100/55) = 18%. That is because only a little over half of those admitting cocaine use within the last 30 days test positive, then almost twice as many of who test positive but do not admit use, are estimated to be actual users within the last 30 days. The back calculation technique cannot be applied to the current data, because the overall sample is small and importantly there were very few individuals who denied use of a drug, but tested positive for that drug. This of course is by design—the present study targeted those willing to admit illicit drug use so that “true” sensitivities of saliva drug screening could be estimated in a field setting. Moreover, individuals willing to admit some illicit drug use might be expected to admit all illicit drug use and also non-medical use of prescription medications—at least to the best of their ability. Thus the low number of positive tests when use of certain drugs was denied is understandable and likely reflects reality. This current study is a “proof of concept” pilot and a larger sample would be required to determine stable and replicable sensitivities for saliva screening. Given that, then the back calculation technique could be used to establish accurate prevalence of illegal and unprescribed drug and medication use among groups of individuals not willing to admit such use in field settings. This will require further research using saliva testing in field settings. Prior research has shown that denial of illicit drug use is common among various at-risk populations. 9 , 10 Consequently it can be recommended that saliva screening should be part of a rapid drug assessment protocol. Footnotes a An estimated 7 out of 25 past 30-day self-reported methadone users/patients were illicit methadone users, according to survey responses and field notes. One of these participants refused to provide a saliva specimen, and of the remaining six, two screened positive for methadone. This relative proportion of illicit methadone users is not high, noting, however, the procedures and non-probability sample employed. Illicit or “street” methadone is often diverted from patients for m