 ] [ Google Scholar ] 135. Tillmann U., John U. Toxic effects of Alexandrium spp. on heterotrophic dinoflagellates: An allelochemical defence mechanism independent of PSP-toxin content. Mar. Ecol. Prog. Ser. 2006;61:47–59. [ Google Scholar ] 136. Juhl A.R., Martins C.A., Anderson D.M. Toxicity of Alexandrium lusitanicum to gastropod larvae is not caused by paralytic-shellfish-poisoning toxins. Harmful Algae. 2008;7:567–573. doi: 10.1016/j.hal.2007.12.019. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 137. Ianora A., Bentley M.G., Caldwell G.S., Casotti R., Cembella A.D., Engstrom-Ost J., Halsband C., Sonnenschein E., Legrand C., Llewellyn C.A., et al. The relevance of marine chemical ecology to plankton and ecosystem function: An emerging field. Mar. Drugs. 2011;9:1625–1648. doi: 10.3390/md9091625. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',90,'2025-10-15 09:37:35','2025-10-15 09:37:35');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4118,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12358240','Role of (18)F-florzolotau PET in diagnostic and therapeutic decision-making for cognitive impairment.','Wang J, Wang ZY, Chen SF, Wang RZ, Chen KL, Lu JY, Xin JW, Huang YY, Wang MY, Xie F, Cheng W, Yen TC, Wischik CM, Cui M, Zuo CT, Zhao QH, Wang YJ, Yu JT.','2025','psychobehavioral huashan huashan avlt osterrieth rocf bnt avft scwt sdmt hama hamd vci nnds huashan biograph umi maximization inspecting bootstrapped resamplings reclassifications vci nnds vci vci vci nnds nnd vci reclassifications vci nnds vci nnds vci achei vci eoad eoad eoad vci vci vci reclassifications ro948 pi2620 tzu aprinoia wischik taurx e70563 e70563 2022zd0211600 2023yfc3605400 e70563 fen cuimei chuan zuochuantao qianhuazhao yanjiang_wang tmmu jintai_yu tagai kubota e48 passamonti leuzy chiotis lemoine apn1607 rabinovici leuzy ro948 marquie siao hoglinger respondek stamelou respondek stamelou chouliaras rabinovici gatsonis hagerstrom pawlik giese hoglinger shimohama tezuka takahata e264 e274 hampel kirmess e228392 brum molfetta barthelemy salvado carlgren gebre rial raghavan e70563 e70563','Abstract INTRODUCTION The novel positron emission tomography (PET) tracer, 18 F‐florzolotau, enables in vivo visualization of tau pathology. METHODS We evaluated the diagnostic performance of disease‐specific spatial patterns of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging by visual read for tauopathies and its added value in diagnostic and therapeutic decision‐making in 1277 participants with cognitive complaints. RESULTS The disease‐specific spatial patterns of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging demonstrated high diagnostic accuracy in differentiating various tauopathies, with 96.0% for Alzheimer''s disease, 94.4% for frontotemporal lobe degeneration, 93.7% for progressive supranuclear palsy, and 97.5% for corticobasal degeneration. Added to a standard diagnostic workup, 18 F‐florzolotau PET reading led to diagnostic revisions for 247 participants (19.3%), increased diagnostic confidence from 68.6% to 81.3%, and resulted in medication changes for 284 participants (22.2%). DISCUSSION Our data advocate for the strategic implementation of 18 F‐florzolotau PET in memory clinics, offering transformative potential for patient care and management. Highlights 18 F‐Florzolotau PET imaging presented disease‐specific spatial patterns for different tauopathies. The disease‐specific spatial patterns of 18 F‐florzolotau positron emission tomography (PET) imaging exhibited high diagnostic accuracy in differentiating various tauopathies. Integration of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging modality to a standard diagnostic workup significantly enhanced diagnostic confidence, enabled diagnostic revisions, and informed adjustments to treatment strategies. Keywords: 18 F‐Florzolotau PET, dementia, diagnosis, tauopathy 1. BACKGROUND Dementia, affecting an estimated 55.2 million individuals worldwide, poses a significant global health challenge. 1 Enhancing the accuracy and timeliness of dementia diagnosis is critical for appropriate clinical management, particularly in the context of tauopathies, a group of neurodegenerative diseases (NDs) characterized by the aggregation of tau protein into filamentous inclusions within neurons and/or glial cells. 2 , 3 Tauopathies can be broadly categorized into primary and secondary forms. Primary tauopathies, such as frontotemporal lobe degeneration (FTLD), progressive supranuclear palsy (PSP), and corticobasal degeneration (CBD), are driven primarily by tau pathology. In contrast, secondary tauopathies involve additional pathogenic factors, exemplified by Alzheimer''s disease (AD), where both amyloid‐beta deposition and tau aggregation play significant roles. 4 From a clinical standpoint, tauopathies often manifest with a complex interplay of cognitive deficits, psychobehavioral abnormalities, and motor disorder, posing a clinical challenge in accurately distinguishing and categorizing these disorders. 4 However, the development of positron emission tomography (PET) ligands targeting tau aggregates has emerged as a key avenue for improving diagnostic precision. The first‐generation tracer, 18 F‐flortaucipir, has received regulatory approval for detecting tau pathology in AD, but its binding capacity to 4R tau isoform in non‐AD primary tauopathies is limited. 5 , 6 A novel secondary‐generation tau tracer, 18 F‐florzolotau, showed high affinity to both 3R and 4R tau isoforms and less off‐target effects, 3 effectively characterizing regional vulnerability in various primary and secondary tauopathies, including AD, FTLD, PSP, and CBD, in vivo in preclinical and small‐scale clinical studies. 3 , 7 , 8 As 18 F‐florzolotau transitions from research to clinical applications, there is a critical need to evaluate its impact on both diagnostic accuracy and patient management in large‐scale clinical studies. Specifically, data are required to determine whether visual interpretation of 18 F‐florzolotau PET scans may enhance the overall patient workflow for individuals presenting with cognitive symptoms, particularly regarding diagnostic revisions, diagnostic confidence, and management strategies. Here, we report the results of a large‐scale, real‐world study designed to address these issues and examine whether 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging can provide clinically actionable insights, both in general and for specific patient subpopulations. 2. METHODS 2.1. Study setting and participants This retrospective cohort study was conducted in memory clinic and neurology clinic at the National Center for Neurological Disorders, Huashan Hospital (Shanghai, China) from January 2019 to July 2024, attracting a large number of patients from all over the country. Eligible patients were required to: (1) have complaints of cognitive decline; (2) completed 18 F‐florzolotau PET scan; (3) completed a standardized diagnostic protocol that included clinical examination, neuropsychological evaluations, routine laboratory tests, and structural magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the brain; (4) willing to participate in this study. Exclusion criteria included: (1) unstable medical or psychiatric conditions; (2) any factors that could compromise the validity or safety of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging procedures; (3) refuse to participate in this study. 18 F‐Florzolotau PET scan was conducted in patients who may benefit from the knowledge of brain tau pathology status determined by the dementia specialist. The study protocol received approval from the Huashan Hospital Ethics Committee. Informed consent was obtained from all participants or their legally authorized representatives prior to enrollment. RESEARCH IN CONTEXT Systematic review : The authors reviewed literature in PubMed related to 18 F‐florzolotau positron emission tomography (PET) for the diagnosis of tauopathies and its added value for clinical diagnosis and treatment in patients with cognitive complaints. A limited number of publications on 18 F‐florzolotau PET were identified. These publications are properly cited throughout the article. Interpretation : Disease‐specific spatial patterns of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging exhibited high diagnostic accuracy in differentiating various tauopathies, and significantly improved the diagnosis, diagnostic confidence, and treatment. Future directions : Our findings advocate for the strategic implementation of 18 F‐florzolotau PET in memory or neurology clinics, offering transformative potential for patient care and management. 2.2. Assessment protocol and diagnosis The assessment protocol is outlined in Figure 1 . All participants underwent a comprehensive diagnostic evaluation, encompassing medical history, clinical examination, neuropsychological assessments, routine laboratory analyses, structural brain MRI, and 18 F‐florzolotau PET scan. All these examinations were completed within 1 month. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Study design. Patients presenting cognitive complaints and completed 18 F‐florzolotau PET scan were recruited following a comprehensive clinical evaluation. The study workup included a clinical examination, cognitive testing, biochemical assays, and structural imaging via MRI. Prior to 18 F‐florzolotau PET scans, neurologists completed a pre‐PET form to document suspected diagnoses, diagnostic confidence levels, and proposed treatment plans. Subsequently, clinicians received the results from the visual interpretation of the 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging and completed a post‐PET form to record any changes in diagnosis, diagnostic confidence, and treatment strategy. MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; PET, positron emission tomography. A comprehensive neuropsychological assessments were administered by three trained personnel who were blinded to the patients’ clinical information, including cognitive function, emotion, sleep, behavioral symptom, and daily function was administered: Mini‐Mental State Examination (MMSE), Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA), Auditory Verbal Learning Test (AVLT), Rey‐Osterrieth Complex Figure Test (ROCF), Boston Naming Test (BNT), Animal Verbal Fluency Test (AVFT), Trail Making Test (TMT), Stroop Color Word Test (SCWT), Symbol Digit Modalities Test (SDMT), Clinical Dementia Rating (CDR) scale, Hamilton Anxiety Scale (HAMA), Hamilton Depression Scale (HAMD), Pittsburgh sleep quality index (PSQI), Neuropsychiatric Inventory‐Questionnaire (NPI‐Q) and Activities of Daily Living (ADL). The patients were classified into subjective cognitive decline (SCD), mild cognitive impairment (MCI), or dementia according to their cognition assessment. The study neurologists were tasked with determining the pre‐PET diagnoses based on clinical, neuropsychological, laboratory, and MRI data. Neurologists concurrently reported their diagnostic confidence, ranging from 0% to 100% in 5% increments, and outlined their intended medication regimens. Upon completion of the pre‐PET assessment, the 18 F‐florzolotau PET results were disclosed to the evaluators, who then conducted a reassessment of the post‐PET diagnosis, diagnostic confidence, and treatment strategies. Both the pre‐ and post‐PET diagnosis referred to the most likely diagnosis (etiology) underlying the cognitive decline, categorized as follows: AD, FTLD, PSP, CBD, dementia with Lewy bodies (DLB), vascular cognitive impairment (VCI), other NDs, or other non‐NDs (NNDs). The pre‐ and post‐PET diagnosis and treatment inform for one patient were determined by the same evaluator. A final diagnosis was independently made by two dementia specialists integrating the above clinical information and other examinations (Aβ‐PET, dopamine transporter [DAT]‐PET, fluorodeoxyglucose [FDG]‐PET, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) tests, gene sequencing, etc.) conducted according to the needs of disease diagnosis and treatment decided by dementia specialist and the patient''s willingness. Any disagreement between the two clinicians was resolved by consulting a third senior specialist. 2.3. 18 F‐Florzolotau PET image assessment 18 F‐Florzolotau PET images were acquired at Huashan Hospital as previous reported 9 and subjected to visual assessment by experienced nuclear medicine physicians blinded to clinical information. Briefly, the dosage of 18 F‐florzolotau for intravenous administration was adjusted according to the patient''s body mass. Image acquisition was conducted 90‐ to 110‐minute post‐tracer injection. All PET scans were performed with either the Biograph mCT Flow PET/CT system (Siemens, Erlangen, Germany) or the uMI 790 PET/MR system (United Imaging Intelligence Co., Ltd., Shanghai, China). Following acquisition, the data underwent reconstruction using a three‐dimensional ordered‐subset expectation maximization algorithm. We chose the whole cerebellum as the reference region in the visual evaluation. That was, the maximum intensity of the evaluated PET scans was first manually adjusted by the rater to ensure that the main color of the whole cerebellum corresponded to the midpoint of the inverted grayscale or spectral scale. 9 The outcomes were initially categorized as either positive or negative. In cases of positive results, the spatial distribution of tau deposition was carefully evaluated to identify disease‐specific patterns (AD, PSP, CBD, FTLD, and inconclusive). 3 Specifically, the characteristic AD pattern was distinguished by tracer accumulation extending from the medial temporal cortex to other neocortical and limbic regions. In contrast, the PSP pattern was marked by enhanced tau deposition in the midbrain, subthalamic nucleus, and adjacent subcortical structures. For CBD, increased tracer accumulation was observed in the primary motor cortex and adjacent cortices, as well as in subcortical regions. In FTLD, the signals were predominantly localized to the frontal and temporal cortices (Figure S1 ). The inconclusive pattern was those that cannot be classified as AD, PSP, CBD, or FTLD. The diagnostic accuracy of 18 F‐florzolotau PET visual assessments was evaluated against the final diagnoses. 2.4. Statistical analyses The normal distribution of continuous data was assessed using the Shapiro–Wilk test and by visually inspecting the Q‐Q plot. Continuous variables were summarized using means and standard deviations (SDs), and intergroup differences were assessed using either the Mann–Whitney U test or the Kruskal–Wallis test, as appropriate. Categorical data were expressed as counts and percentages and compared using the χ 2 test. Diagnostic metrics, including accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value (PPV), and negative predictive value (NPV), were calculated as means with 95% confidence intervals (CIs) derived from a bootstrapped 1000 resamplings, using the final diagnosis as the reference standard. To evaluate changes following 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging, we employed the McNemar test for paired categorical data and the paired Wilcoxon signed rank test for continuous variables. To determine which participant groups had more significant diagnostic revisions, subgroup analyses were performed based on cognitive impairment stage (SCD, MCI, and dementia), and pre‐ 18 F‐florzolotau PET diagnostic confidence levels (≤70% vs. > 70%). Additionally, univariate and multivariable logistic regression analyses were performed to identify factors associated with diagnostic reclassifications. Variables with a p value < 0.1 in univariate logistic analysis, as determined by the likelihood ratio test, were entered into the final multivariable model. Analyses were performed using R, version 4.2.3, with all tests two‐sided at a 5% level of significance. 3. RESULTS 3.1. Participant characteristics The general characteristics of the 1277 study patients are summarized in Table 1 and Table S1 . Concerning cognitive stages, 88 participants (6.9%) were diagnosed with SCD, 493 (38.6%) with MCI, and 696 (54.5%) with dementia. Before 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging was conducted, neurologists clinically diagnosed 684 participants (53.6%) with AD, 92 (7.2%) with FTLD, 29 (2.3%) with dementia with Lewy body (DLB), 173 (13.5%) with PSP, and 23 (1.8%) with CBD. Additionally, 139 patients (10.9%) were identified with other NDs, 30 (2.3%) with VCI, and 107 (8.4%) with other non‐NDs (NNDs). TABLE 1. General characteristics of the study participants. Characteristic Entire cohort SCD MCI Dementia Patient number 1277 88 493 696 Age at onset, mean (SD), years 61.54 (9.66) 56.14 (10.78) 63.47 (9.50) 60.85 (9.27) Disease duration, mean (SD), years 2.65 (1.89) 2.56 (2.62) 2.38 (2.02) 2.86 (1.64) Education, mean (SD), y 10.47 (4.14) 13.81 (3.83) 11.02 (3.85) 9.74 (4.15) Female sex, n (%) 657 (51.4) 39 (44.3) 235 (47.7) 383 (55.0) MMSE score, mean (SD) 19.47 (7.75) 28.60 (0.92) 25.55 (2.56) 14.50 (6.68) Pre‐ 18 F‐florzolotau PET diagnosis, n (%) AD 684 (53.6) 19 (21.6) 261 (52.9) 404 (58.0) FTD 92 (7.2) 1 (1.1) 14 (2.8) 77 (11.1) VCI 30 (2.3) 0 (0.0) 9 (1.8) 21 (3.0) DLB 29 (2.3) 0 (0.0) 5 (1.0) 24 (3.4) PSP 173 (13.5) 6 (6.8) 72 (14.6) 95 (13.6) CBD 23 (1.8) 0 (0.0) 6 (1.2) 17 (2.4) Other NDs 139 (10.9) 33 (37.5) 82 (16.6) 24 (3.4) Other non‐NDs 107 (8.4) 29 (33.0) 44 (8.9) 34 (4.9) Positive 18 F‐florzolotau PET findings, n (%) 986 (77.2) 45 (51.1) 332 (67.3) 609 (87.5) Pre‐ 18 F‐florzolotau PET diagnostic confidence, mean (SD), % 68.61 (9.36) 69.77 (10.39) 68.16 (8.66) 68.78 (9.69) Open in a new tab Note : Data are presented as mean (SD) for continuous variables and counts (proportions, %) for categorical variables. Abbreviations: AD, Alzheimer''s disease; CBD, corticobasal degeneration; DLB, dementia with Lewy bodies; FTD, frontotemporal dementia; MCI, mild cognitive impairment; MMSE, Mini‐Mental State Examination; MoCA, Montreal Cognitive Assessment; NDs, neurodegenerative diseases; PET, positron emission tomography; PSP, progressive supranuclear palsy; SCD, subjective cognitive decline; VCI, vascular cognitive impairment. 3.2. 18 F‐Florzolotau PET imaging patterns in relation to pre‐PET classification The study design was shown in Figure 1 and the representative images of 18 F‐florzolotau PET disease‐specific patterns were shown in Figure S1 . In the entire cohort, 986 out of 1277 participants (77.2%) exhibited positive 18 F‐florzolotau PET findings – with rates of 48.9% in those with SCD, 66.7% in individuals with MCI, and 86.5% in those with dementia (Figure S2 ). Based on the pre‐PET classification, the percentages of positive tau PET were 81.9% for AD, 70.7% for FTLD, 91.3% for PSP, 78.3% for CBD, 46.7% for VCI, 69% for DLB, 77.7% for other NDs, and 40.2% for other NNDs. On analyzing 18 F‐florzolotau PET patterns, 525 of the 684 (76.8%) patients with an pre‐PET classification of AD presented a typical AD pattern, whereas 103 of the 173 (59.5%) PSP cases demonstrated a characteristic PSP pattern. Conversely, only 13 of the 92 (14.1%) patients with a pre‐PET diagnosis of FTLD exhibited an FTLD pattern, and 5 of the 23 (21.7%) with CBD showed a CBD pattern. 3.3. Diagnostic accuracy of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging in differentiating tauopathies We subsequently evaluated the diagnostic performance of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging in differentiating various tauopathies based on the final diagnosis (Figure 2 ). For AD discrimination, the presence of an AD pattern demonstrated remarkable accuracy (96.0%), with high sensitivity (96.0%), and specificity (96.0%). The PPV and NPV were 95.6% and 96.4%, respectively. In distinguishing PSP, the PSP pattern exhibited moderate sensitivity (64.4%) but high specificity (98.5%). The PPV, NPV, and overall accuracy for PSP were 87.0%, 94.5%, and 93.7%, respectively. For FTLD and CBD, the FTLD pattern and CBD pattern demonstrated low sensitivity (19.5% for FTLD, 41.7% for CBD) but high specificity (99.8% for FTLD and 98.6% for CBD). The overall accuracy were for 94.4% FTLD and 97.5% for CBD. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Discriminative accuracy of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging in patients with cognitive complaints. (A) Proportion of each underlying etiology assigned after 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging. (B) Concordance analysis between the patterns observed in 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging and the clinical diagnoses made considering PET findings. (C) Diagnostic metrics illustrating the accuracy of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging patterns in discriminating tauopathies from other NDs. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; CBD, corticobasal degeneration; DLB, dementia with Lewy bodies; FTLD, frontotemporal dementia; NDs, neurodegenerative diseases; NND, non‐neurodegenerative diseases; NPV, negative predictive value; PET, positron emission tomography; PPV, positive predictive value; PSP, progressive supranuclear palsy; VCI, vascular cognitive impairment. 3.4. Impact of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging on diagnostic revisions The analysis of 18 F‐florzolotau PET findings led to diagnostic revisions in 19.3% of participants (247 out of 1277; McNemar test, p < 0.001) (Figure 3 ). Notably, a negative 18 F‐florzolotau PET scan contributed to a higher proportion of diagnostic reclassifications (43.0%, 125 out of 291) compared to a positive visual read (12.4%, 122 out of 986) (Table S2 ). The highest frequency of diagnostic revisions occurred in patients with MCI (25.2%), followed by SCD (20.5%), and dementia (15.1%). When stratified by pre‐PET diagnosis, the conditions with the highest rates of diagnostic revisions after imaging were VCI (36.7%), FTLD (30.4%), DLB (27.6%), other non‐NDs (27.1%), and AD (20.3%) (Figure 4 ). FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Diagnostic revisions induced by 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging findings in the entire study cohort. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; CBD, corticobasal degeneration; DLB, dementia with Lewy bodies; FTLD, frontotemporal dementia; NDs, neurodegenerative disease; NNDs, non‐neurodegenerative diseases; PET, positron emission tomography; PSP, progressive supranuclear palsy; VCI, vascular cognitive impairment. FIGURE 4. Open in a new tab Diagnostic revisions based on 18 F‐florzolotau PET findings. The left column lists the initial clinical diagnoses, whereas each row shows the revised diagnoses following the interpretation of PET scans. AD, Alzheimer''s disease; CBD, corticobasal degeneration; DLB, dementia with Lewy bodies; FTLD, frontotemporal dementia; NDs, neurodegenerative diseases; NNDs, non‐neurodegenerative diseases; PET, positron emission tomography; PSP, progressive supranuclear palsy; VCI, vascular cognitive impairment. 3.5. Effect of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging on diagnostic confidence 18 F‐Florzolotau PET scans significantly enhanced overall diagnostic confidence, with mean values increasing from 68.6% (SD 9.4%) before imaging to 81.3% (SD 11.5%) ( p < 0.001) thereafter (Figure 5 ). Positive PET results yielded a more substantial increase in diagnostic confidence (69.4% [SD 9.4] to 83.3% [SD 10.1]) compared to negative findings (66.1% [SD 8.7] to 74.5% [SD 13.3]; p < 0.001). Across cognitive impairment categories, the most pronounced improvement in diagnostic confidence was observed in patients with dementia (68.8% [SD 9.7] to 82.7% [SD 11.8]), followed by those with MCI (68.2% [SD 8.7] to 79.6% [SD 11.2]), and SCD (69.8% [SD 10.4] to 79.4% [SD 10.2]). The AD (69.9% [SD 8.1] to 87.2% [SD 6.7]) and FTLD (65.3% [SD 9.2] to 81.3% [SD 8.0]) patterns contributed to a significantly greater increase in diagnostic confidence compared to an inconclusive pattern (69.7% [SD 11.3] to 75.7% [SD 11.2]) (Figure S3 and Table S3 ). FIGURE 5. Open in a new tab Changes in diagnostic confidence after 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging, evaluated across the entire cohort and stratified by cognitive stages. The figure illustrates the changes in diagnostic confidence, calculated as the difference between post‐PET and pre‐PET confidence levels. The results are presented for the entire cohort (A) and the following specific subgroups: (B) SCD;(C) MCI; (D) dementia. MCI, mild cognitive impairment; PET, positron emission tomography; SCD, subjective cognitive decline. 3.6. Modifications in clinical management 18 F‐Florzolotau PET findings led to modifications in clinical management in 22.2% (284/1277) of the study participants (McNemar test, p < 0.001), with varying impacts across cognitive impairment categories (SCD, 20.5% [18/88]; MCI, 21.9% [108/493]; dementia, 22.7% [158/696]). In the entire study cohort, imaging results prompted substantial changes in pharmacological interventions, including the introduction or discontinuation of acetylcholinesterase inhibitors in 10.9% ( n = 139) of patients, memantine in 5.0% ( n = 64), and other psychotropic medications (e.g., sedatives, antidepressants, and antipsychotics) in 11.0% ( n = 140). A minimal impact on motor symptom‐related medications was observed, with only 21 of 1277 patients (1.6%) experiencing alterations in their prescriptions following the 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging (Figure 6 ). FIGURE 6. Open in a new tab Modifications in clinical management induced by 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging findings in the entire study cohort. AChEI, acetyl‐cholinesterase inhibitors; MCI, mild cognitive impairment; SCD, subjective cognitive decline. 3.7. Subgroup analyses We further conducted subgroup analyses to identify specific patient populations that may derive the greatest benefit from 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging. Univariable logistic regression analysis revealed that older age at onset, cognitive status at MCI level, and lower pre‐PET diagnostic confidence were significantly associated with diagnostic revisions. These results were further confirmed after adjustment for potential confounders in multivariable logistic regression analysis (Table 2 ). A subgroup analysis was performed based on pre‐PET diagnostic confidence levels, using the median value of 70% as the threshold. In the subgroup with higher (>70%) pre‐PET diagnostic confidence, diagnoses were revised in 4.5% (17/375) of patients. In contrast, in the subgroup with lower (≤70%) diagnostic confidence, changes occurred in 25.5% (230/902) of cases. Within the lower confidence subgroup, the pre‐PET diagnoses with the highest rates of revision were VCI (47.8%), FTLD (38.4%), DLB (33.3%), other non‐NDs (28.7%), and AD (25.8%). For patients with pre‐PET diagnoses of PSP, CBD, and other NDs, the rates of diagnostic reclassification after 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging were 9.2%, 17.4% and 8.6%, respectively. In the subgroup with higher diagnostic confidence, 15.4% of patients who had a pre‐PET diagnosis of other non‐NDs experienced a diagnostic revision after 18 F‐florzolotau PET. For other baseline diagnoses with more than 10 patients, the percentage of diagnostic reclassification was 0–5.9% (Table S4 ). TABLE 2. Logistic regression analysis: Factors associated with diagnostic revisions. Beta SE Odds ratio 95% CI p ‐Value Univariable logistic regression analysis Age at onset 0.0024 0.0011 1.002 (1.000,1.005) 0.031 Female sex −0.0316 0.0221 0.969 (0.928,1.012) 0.153 Education −0.0019 0.0036 0.998 (0.991,1.005) 0.597 Cognitive status at MCI level a 0.1007 0.0231 1.106 (1.057,1.157) <0.001 Cognitive status at SCD level a 0.0537 0.0444 1.055 (0.967,1.151) 0.227 Pre‐PET diagnostic confidence −0.0112 0.0011 0.989 (0.987,0.991) <0.001 Disease duration −0.0017 0.0059 0.998 (0.987,1.010) 0.766 Multivariable logistic regression analysis Age at onset 0.0033 0.0011 1.003 (1.001,1.005) 0.003 Cognitive status at MCI level a 0.0847 0.0225 1.088 (1.041,1.137) <0.001 Pre‐PET diagnostic confidence −0.0114 0.0011 0.989 (0.986,0.991) <0.001 Open in a new tab Note : Factors with a p value < 0.1 in the univariable logistic analysis were entered into the multivariable logistic regression analysis. Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; MCI, mild cognitive impairment; MRI, magnetic resonance imaging; PET, positron emission tomography; SCD, subjective cognitive decline; SE, standard error. a Compared to cognitive status at dementia level. In addition, we compared the roles of tau PET for diagnostic and therapeutic decision‐making in early‐onset AD (EOAD) and late‐onset AD (LOAD) patients, and found that 18 F‐florzolotau PET resulted in higher rate of diagnostic revision (EOAD vs. LOAD: 16.5% vs. 25.6%, p < 0.05), less increase in diagnostic confidence (16.9% vs. 12.9%, p < 0.05), and higher rate of medication changes (32.6% vs. 41.1%, p < 0.05) in LOAD than in EOAD (Tables S5–S8 ). Higher rate of diagnostic revision and medication changes, and more increase in diagnostic confidence were observed in AD versus non‐AD group (diagnostic revision: 20.3% vs. 11.0%, increased diagnostic confidence: 15.2% vs. 9.8%, medication: 36.1% vs. 19.7%, p < 0.001) (Table S9 ). 4. DISCUSSION In this cohort study, we assessed the utility and impact of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging in distinguishing various tauopathies, and its influence on diagnostic and therapeutic decisions. The visual interpretation of 18 F‐florzolotau PET scans demonstrated high accuracy across multiple tauopathies, achieving 96.0% for AD, 93.7% for PSP, 94.4% for FTLD, and 97.5% for CBD. The inclusion of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging in the diagnostic workup had a significant clinical impact, including diagnostic revisions for 19.3% of participants, an enhancement in overall diagnostic confidence from 68.6% to 81.3%, and alterations in clinical management for 22.2% of the study patients. The incremental value of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging is much higher in pre‐PET diagnosed AD group than non‐AD group. In the evaluation of the diagnostic performance of 18 F‐florzolotau PET, clinical diagnostic metrics demonstrated significant variability in relation to the prevalence of disease and tau pathology. AD is the most common cause of dementia, comprised 47% of all cases in the present study. 18 F‐Florzolotau PET exhibited diagnostic accuracy of 96.0% in presence of an AD pattern with both high sensitivity and specificity, similar to other tau PET tracers. 10 , 11 , 12 For PSP and CBD, which only accounts for 14% and 2% in the present study, 18 F‐florzolotau PET exhibited high specificity, coupled with moderate sensitivity, indicating 18 F‐florzolotau as a promising tracer for PSP diagnosis, differential diagnosis, and disease severity assessment. 8 , 13 FTLD are characterized by low prevalence and less than 50% of FTLD are pathologically tau‐positive. 14 Thus, 18 F‐florzolotau PET showed very low sensitivity of 19.5%, but the PPV was relatively high (89.5%). In the current study, visual interpretation of 18 F‐florzolotau PET results led to diagnostic revisions in 19.3% of the study participants. In clinical practice, particularly in memory clinics, AD frequently presents with symptoms that are difficult to distinguish from those of VCI, FTLD, depression, and other non‐ND (e.g., sleep disorders, certain infections, and nutritional deficiencies). 4 Consistently, our study revealed that participants initially diagnosed with AD predominantly had their diagnoses revised to other non‐ND, FTLD, or VCI following 18 F‐florzolotau PET. Conversely, patients with pre‐PET diagnoses of FTLD or VCI were frequently reclassified as AD following PET imaging. Furthermore, we found that diagnostic reclassifications were more prevalent in patients exhibiting cognitive impairment at the MCI stage, those with lower baseline diagnostic confidence, and later age at onset. Besides, integration of 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging also contributed to more diagnostic revision and medication changes in AD than in non‐AD, especially in LOAD. 18 F‐Florzolotau PET imaging may therefore be particularly valuable in such patient subgroups, where the examination is more likely to influence clinical decision‐making and potentially yield greater patient benefit. However, the advanced disease stage of numerous patients with motor symptoms (e.g., PSP) at initial evaluation likely enhanced pre‐imaging diagnostic accuracy. 15 , 16 Furthermore, the underrepresentation of atypical non‐AD tauopathies in the study cohort may have influenced diagnostic patterns. 17 Despite this limitation, 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging enhanced overall diagnostic confidence. Negative or inconclusive imaging findings can reduce confidence, as adjusting diagnoses based on ambiguous results is challenging. 16 However, while negative tau PET readings prompted a higher number of diagnostic revisions, positive results reinforced diagnostic certainty. Following 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging, 22.2% of the study patients underwent modifications to their medication regimens due to revised diagnoses. While this rate is lower than the previously reported 36%–45% alteration in AD drug use after amyloid PET scans, 18 , 19 our results align with prior observations that the most significant treatment adjustments occurred in patients with positive scans. 20 Additionally, 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging had minimal impact on the use of motor‐related drugs, likely because these medications are more symptom‐specific and less influenced by diagnostic changes. 21 A previous study has reported that including 18 F‐RO948 tau PET in the diagnostic workup resulted in a change in diagnosis in 7.5% of participants and a change in medication in 5.5% of patients with cognitive symptoms from secondary memory clinics. 20 In their study, CSF AD biomarkers (Aβ42, Aβ40, and p‐tau181) were also included for the pre‐PET diagnosis, resulting a lower revision rate for the diagnosis and management than those in our present study. In clinical practice, physicians often choose either CSF tests or PET scans considering the costs. Another study reported that the combination of Aβ and tau PETs was associated with changes in management and diagnosis of cognitive impairment, and the 18 F‐PI2620 tau PET had stronger associations with change management than Aβ. 22 The above and our present study together support the role of tau PET in diagnostic and therapeutic decision‐making for cognitive impairment. The need for accurate and cost‐effective diagnostic methods has become increasingly critical. While CSF AD biomarkers are gaining global traction due to their high diagnostic accuracy and relatively low cost, the invasive nature of lumbar punctures often leads to patient reluctance. 23 Blood tests are promising with an accuracy >90% (particularly p‐tau217) in detecting cerebral amyloid pathology and with the minimal invasive and low cost. 24 , 25 , 26 , 27 However, current blood biomarkers cannot reflect the temporal and spatial distribution of pathologies in the brain and cannot be used in the differential diagnosis of various tauopathies. Consequently, PET imaging remains necessary to confirm blood biomarker results and serve as a viable alternative for determining underlying pathologies. 28 , 29 Notably, tau PET has demonstrated superior capability compared to amyloid PET in monitoring disease severity and predicting disease progression. 30 , 31 Previous research also supports the use of tau PET for confirming or excluding AD in patients with MCI and for identifying AD pathology in those with SCD. 11 , 28 Based on these findings, 18 F‐florzolotau PET imaging may be valuable in MCI patients who present with ambiguous symptoms that result in low diagnostic confidence. There are several limitations to this study. First, despite the relatively sizable cohort, the number of patients in specific subgroups, such as FTLD and CBD, was limited due to the low prevalence of these conditions. This caveat introduces caution when interpreting the results, as the statistical power and representativeness may be compromised. Second, the final diagnoses in this study were not confirmed by definitive post mortem pathology, although other examinations have been considered. Third, the study population was drawn from a single center in China, resulting in an ethnically homogeneous sample. This raises concerns about the study''s generalizability to diverse populations. Finally, 18 F‐florzolotau PET results were determined through visual assessment rather than quantitative analysis. Although visual reading methods have been reported to yield good sensitivity and specificity, 32 the integration of artificial intelligence based tools to analyze tau distribution patterns and quantify tau burden could provide a more precise, objective, and standardized diagnostic approach. 33 In conclusion, our findings highlight the high accuracy of 18 F‐florzolotau PET in differentiating tauopathies, emphasizing its substantial added value to standard clinical assessments for individuals presenting with cognitive impairments. The integration of this imaging modality not only significantly enhanced diagnostic confidence but also enabled diagnostic revisions and informed adjustments to treatment strategies. Collectively, our data advocate for the strategic implementation of 18 F‐florzolotau PET in memory and neurology clinics, offering transformative potential for patient care and management. CONFLICT OF INTERES