Service (NITOS). Harvey L Neiman Health Policy Institute 2015. https://www.neimanhpi.org/neiman-imaging-types-of-service-nitos/ (accessed July 29, 2020). 21. Rubin GD, Ryerson CJ, Haramati LB. The role of chest imaging in patient management during the COVID-19 pandemic: a multinational consensus statement from the Fleischner Society. Chest. 2020;158:106–116. doi: 10.1016/j.chest.2020.04.003. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Lang M, Yeung T, Mendoza DP. Imaging volume trends and recovery during the COVID-19 pandemic: a comparative analysis between a large urban academic hospital and its affiliated imaging centers. Acad Radiol. 2020;27:1353–1362. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2020.08.008. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Delayed Cancer Screenings. Epic Health Research Network 2020. https://www.ehrn.org/delays-in-preventive-cancer-screenings-during-covid-19-pandemic/ (accessed August 3, 2020). 24. Delayed Cancer Screenings—A Second Look – Epic Health Research Network. Epic Health Research Network 2020. https://ehrn.org/delayed-cancer-screenings-a-second-look/ (accessed July 30, 2020). 25. Vagal A, Mahoney M, Anderson JL. Recover Wisely from COVID-19: responsible resumption of nonurgent radiology services. Acad Radiol. 2020;27:1343–1352. doi: 10.1016/j.acra.2020.08.002. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis, Economic Research. Unemployment Rate 2020. https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/UNRATE (accessed July 29, 2020).',19,'2025-10-15 09:38:06','2025-10-15 09:38:06');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(4127,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12290034','Non-invasive imaging modalities for diagnosing pulsatile tinnitus: a comprehensive review and recommended imaging algorithm.','Duvvuri M, Ali H, Amans MR.','2025','shunting heartbeat fibromuscular encephalocele otosclerosis adjudicator shunting shunting pitched vss iih iih iih vss proptosis tortuosity sella emissary iih expansile aplastic thrombosed stenotic davf tricks vss turbulent stenotic arachnoid granulations deflection tentorium iih iih stenotic stenotic davfs vss 40s audible bruit tricks shunting patulous shunting 30s flattened sella laterally 60s outpouching tricks outpouching outpouching 20s spgr outpouching mesotympanum shunting shunting davfs davfs davf davf adept davfs shunting shunting davfs davf davfs davfs tricks tricks davf davf tricks davfs asymmetrically davfs shaggy transcalvarial collaterals davfs davf pitched davfs resistive edv davfs davf davf versed resistive resistive davf avms turbulent tricks avms nidus machet tricks avms nidus feeders avms avms ccfs shunting airbags seatbelts ccfs steno bruit stenotic tricks coincident unrevealing brachiocephalic 50s brachiocephalic brachiocephalic fibromuscular 70s tricks tortuosity irregularly reformat tricks tricks beaded spgr fibromuscular tricks iih emissary diverticula emissary emissary emissary petrosquamosal stenotic turbulent turbulent emissary riding cfd riding riding cranially petrous riding diverticula spgr outpouching diverticula cfd turbulent jets stapedial depositions turbulence aplasia stapedial stapedial spinosum stapedial retrotympanic aica cerebellopontine impinge vestibulocochlear fiesta cerebellopontine fiesta intracochlear avidly spgr intracochlear intracochlear 30s fiesta intracochlear encephalocele arachnoid 40s fiesta arachnoid encephalocele arachnoid narrows glomus tympanicum promontory avidly proclivity glomus jugulare hemangiopericytomas intracochlear sswd iih scrutinizing riding aqueducts aqueduct aqueduct aqueducts congenitally aqueducts aqueduct aqueducts normalcy iih otosclerosis otospongiosis hypodensity fissulae antefenestrum fenestral otospongiosis fibro expansile otic demineralization intracochlear compresses audiologic turbulent epitympanum prussak ossicles erosive scutum ossicles encephaloceles iih spgr encephaloceles protruding protruding epitympanum ossicles herniations arachnoid granulations bhag iih encephaloceles spgr herniations tricks neurointerventionalists iih davfs supp1 nihms2031067 supp1 t32eb001631 amans mattay abdalkader norbash shweel hamdy lingam arteriography formeister hwa cavarocchi e030 cummins caton jon cummins caton hemphill neurointerventional jnis mattox fountain arteriography laryngologica harnsberger grierson bou haidar dumper narsinh saloner amans jon nossek jethanamest e000154 svin sundararajan kishore a6890 sakata fushimi shunts s00234 escott patsalides stenotic neuros nyab222 stenotic laryngologica s00234 narsinh duvvuri neurointervent neurintsurg narvid blevins a2328 ultrasmedbio jeng sichtermann faron sijben a5911 machet portefaix kadziolka s00234 sonmez basekim clinimag mattox hudgins laryngologica sismanis daneshi hadizadeh mahmoudian eyk s12938 gottesman 0b013e3182675511 dicks gornik fibromuscular fibromuscular e021962 mundada lingam arteriography otoscopic amans haraldsson a5833 essibayi oushy lanzino neuros nyab299 kefayati amans jbiomech pegge steens s40134 bathla nagpal jcis_196_2020 mitton killeen dawes pjd basiouny s0022215100145293 madani sej s00234 zhaohui s00234 angster dewan wippold jec aqueduct papanikolaou bibas ferekidis encephalocele caton encephalocele arachnoid granulations s00234 supp1 nihms2031067 supp1','Abstract Pulsatile tinnitus (PT) is a challenging diagnostic condition arising from various vascular, neoplastic, and systemic disorders. Non-invasive imaging is essential for identifying underlying causes while minimizing risks of invasive diagnostic angiography. Although no consensus exists on the primary imaging modality for PT and currently CT, Ultrasound and MRI are used in the diagnostic pathway, MRI is increasingly preferred as the first line screening test for its diagnostic efficacy and safety. MRI protocols such as Time-of-Flight (ToF), Magnetic Resonance Angiography (MRA), Diffusion Weighted Imaging (DWI), and Arterial Spin Labeling (ASL) can identify serious causes, including vascular shunting lesions, venous sinus stenosis, and tumors. In this narrative review of the current literature, we discuss the benefits and limitations of various non-invasive imaging modalities in identifying the characteristic imaging findings of the most common causes of pulsatile tinnitus and also provide an algorithm which clinicians can use to guide the imaging evaluation. Introduction Pulsatile tinnitus (PT), a rhythmic auditory sensation synchronous with the patient’s heartbeat, presents a diagnostic challenge due to its potential association with various vascular, neoplastic, metabolic, and systemic disorders. In addition to having a potentially dangerous cause, PT is often quite debilitating for patients [ 1 ]. In ascertaining the etiology of pulsatile tinnitus, non-invasive imaging has emerged as a cornerstone in the diagnostic process, offering insights into underlying causes without the risks associated with invasive procedures. Currently, there is no universally accepted consensus regarding the primary imaging modality for assessing the cause of PT. Additionally, there are no studies that directly compare imaging modalities to determine which is the single best screening study to ascertain the etiology of a patient’s pulsatile tinnitus. While the American Academy of Otolaryngology and the American College of Radiology (ACR) have yet to recommend a primary imaging study, multiple literature sources advocate for either computed tomography (CT) or magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) as the initial investigative tool[ 2 – 5 ], a single meta-analysis conducted in this field was inconclusive in recommending a first-line imaging modality[ 6 ], and a physiology-based protocol for patient evaluation using MRI has been defined[ 7 ]. Based on the analysis of the current literature, in this review, we recommend MRI/MRA as the first-line imaging for patients presenting with PT. There is a general agreement that digital subtraction angiography (DSA), while highly detailed, should not be employed as the first line of screening but reserved for cases where high-risk cerebrovascular pathologies are suspected based on non-invasive imaging, or if patients are disabled by their symptoms and a potentially treatable cause is identified on the non-invasive imaging[ 8 ]. This narrative review of the literature evaluates the benefits and limitations of various non-invasive imaging modalities in assessing the individual causes of pulsatile tinnitus. Additionally, in this review, we discuss the specific imaging findings that physicians should be looking for to help identify underlying diagnoses on each of the imaging modalities. Finally, we provide a recommended algorithm for imaging patients that takes into account findings on history and physical examination, findings on prior imaging studies, and availability of resources. Methods Literature Search Strategy and Selection Criteria For this review, a literature search was performed in October 2023 using three databases (PubMed, Google Scholar and Web of Science) without a publication date limit. All full-text, English language publications were queried using the following key words: “pulsatile tinnitus” + “(imaging type)”, as well as “pulsatile tinnitus” + “non-invasive imaging” + “(cause of pulsatile tinnitus)”. For “imaging type”, keywords included: “MRI”, “CT”, “Ultrasound”, “CT temporal bone”. For “cause of pulsatile tinnitus”, keywords included: “dural arteriovenous fistulas”, “schwannoma”, “paraganglioma”, “tumors”, “arteriovenous malformations”, “venous sinus stenosis”, “idiopathic intracranial hypertension”, “sigmoid sinus dehiscence”, “jugular bulb abnormalities”, “temporal bone”, “fibromuscular dysplasia”, “aneurysms”, “carotid cavernous fistulas”, “venous sinus stenosis”, “encephalocele”, “cholesteatoma” and “otosclerosis”. After removing duplicates, two reviewers screened titles and abstracts (MD, HA) from the search results using the following inclusion criteria: 1) Pulsatile tinnitus had to be the presenting symptom and 2) a non-invasive diagnostic imaging modality had to be used to evaluate PT. In case of disagreement between the two initial reviewers, a third reviewer (MRA) served as adjudicator. Prior to full-text review, references of included reviews were screened for additional articles for review. Case reports, book chapters, commentaries, and editorials were excluded. Studies that discussed surgical outcomes or invasive imaging modalities such as digital subtraction angiography were excluded. Data Extraction Each study was then reviewed in full to assess the details of the diagnostic imaging modality used, results of diagnostic work up using this imaging modality and diagnostic yield. The PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) flow diagram is provided in the supplemental figures . Non-Invasive Imaging for Diagnosing the Etiology of Pulsatile Tinnitus First-line imaging with MRI The choice of the primary imaging modality for PT must be informed by both the clinical presentation and the need to prioritize detection of the most dangerous causes, such as tumors and dangerous vascular lesions that can lead to hemorrhage ( Figure 1 ). The literature increasingly supports MRI as the preferred first-line tool due to its comprehensive capability to detect a wide range of pathologies without radiation exposure. Some authors have advocated for the use of a single arteriovenous phase CT protocol or a dual-phase contrast enhanced CT to interrogate for vascular and osseous causes of PT[ 4 , 9 ]. Overall, however in detecting etiologies of PT that not only require expectant management and treatment, such as tumors and vascular shunting lesions but also benign etiologies, MRI has been shown to have higher sensitivity when compared to other imaging modalities[ 10 – 12 ]. An MRI protocol comprising of Time-of-Flight (ToF), Magnetic Resonance Angiography (MRA), Diffusion Weighted Imaging (DWI), Arterial Spin Labeling (ASL), and T1-weighted sequences both with and without contrast to reliably identify the most serious causes of PT[ 7 , 13 ]. Additionally, when considering pediatric patients or conditions where multiple follow-up scans may be necessary, the non-ionizing nature of MRI is a significant advantage[ 14 ]. Given the compelling evidence of MRI’s comprehensive diagnostic capabilities, its safety profile, and its cost-effectiveness when compared to other modalities, it is advocated as the initial diagnostic modality for patients presenting with PT. This approach aligns with the aim to first rule out the most dangerous causes of PT, thereby ensuring patient safety while optimizing diagnostic efficiency. However, if there is clinical concern for dangerous causes of PT due to an arterial or shunting lesion, DSA should be performed even if the MRI/MRA is normal. Clinically concerning symptoms of PT include improvement of PT with occipital artery compression, high-pitched pulse synchronous sound, and additional neurological symptoms or a focal neurologic deficit [ 8 ]. Figure 1: Recommended algorithm for using non-invasive imaging to evaluate for cause of pulsatile tinnitus. Open in a new tab MRI/MRA is recommended as the first-line modality for evaluating for cause of PT. In settings where MRI is unavailable or MRI is contraindicated, CT/CTA/CTV can be used for evaluation. In cases where the MRI is normal but clinical suspicion for a high-risk lesion is persistent, invasive imaging with a DSA, balloon occlusion test and venous manometry is indicated. Venous Sinus Stenosis Venous sinus stenosis (VSS) has been identified as the most common cause of PT in a large cohort study[ 15 ]. Idiopathic intracranial hypertension (IIH) is one of the most common etiologies of venous sinus stenosis, and its diagnosis is largely contingent on the effective use of non-invasive imaging modalities. Distinguishing clinical symptoms of patients presenting with IIH include headache, PT, papilledema, and elevated ICP, which require urgent management. On MRI with and without contrast and Magnetic Resonance Venography (MRV), characteristic imaging findings of IIH can include VSS, globe proptosis, marked optic nerve tortuosity, empty or partially empty sella, or prominence of the emissary veins[ 16 ]. CT in general has a lower sensitivity for IIH [ 13 ]. However, MRV or CTV can accurately assess venous patency, to first exclude dural venous sinus thrombosis, especially in the emergent setting. On CTV, an expansile filling defect in the venous sinus can indicate a dural venous sinus thrombosis, with possible corresponding hemorrhagic infarcts. On MRV, loss of flow-related artifacts along the venous sinuses can indicate thrombus. However, it should be noted that loss of flow related signal on ToF MRV is non-specific and may indicate an aplastic or hypoplastic sinus, thrombosed sinus, severely stenotic sinus or DAVF. ToF MRA and TRICKS can more accurately assess venous anatomy, depicting the hemodynamic abnormalities characteristic of VSS.[ 8 , 11 , 15 – 17 ]. Most commonly affecting the transverse sinuses, an abrupt change in venous caliber of a dominant venous sinus can result in turbulent flow, which can manifest as PT ( Figure 2 ). 4D Flow MRI sequences can also be used to determine a trans-stenotic blood flow velocity difference that correlates with cerebral venous pressure gradient, specifically in transverse venous sinus stenosis[ 20 ]. Using contrast-enhanced MRV reconstructed images, intrinsic versus extrinsic causes of venous sinus stenosis can be determined. Arachnoid granulations can cause intrinsic stenosis whereas compression by adjacent brain parenchyma or deflection of the tentorium can cause extrinsic stenosis. In a cohort studying IIH and PT patients, extrinsic stenosis was seen in 65% of IIH patients and intrinsic stenosis was seen in 85% of PT patients [ 16 ]. Where MRI is unavailable, CTV and post-processing can also be used to identify venous sinus stenosis and also predict the trans-stenotic gradient across the stenosis[ 21 ]. 3D CTA reconstructions where the ratio between the vessels’ largest and smallest cross-sectional areas were computed can also be helpful in determining the presence of stenosis[ 22 ]. Determining trans-stenotic pressure gradients using non-invasive imaging can be helpful for management and treatment planning while offering a non-invasive alternative to invasive pressure measurements using DSA. Figure 2: Venous etiologies of pulsatile tinnitus. Patients with DAVFs and VSS and present with papilledema are at higher risk. (A-E) Cervical spine epidural arteriovenous fistula. Open in a new tab A patient in their 40s presented with right greater than left PT and an audible bruit on the right side. (A) Coronal MIP Of TRICKS MRA demonstrates marked venous shunting along the right and to a lesser extent left paravertebral cervical spine, with early venous opacification of multiple enlarged venous structures (white arrow). (B) Axial ToF MRA of the cervical spine demonstrates abnormal opacification of a paravertebral vein (white arrow) adjacent to the distal V2 segment of the right vertebral artery at the C2-C3 level (curved white arrow). (C) Axial ASL shows abnormal signal in the right paravertebral region of the cervical spine (white arrow). (D, E) Injection of the right vertebral artery shows a patulous distal V2 segment with a connection between the right vertebral artery and the right paravertebral vein at C2–3 with marked paravertebral shunting involving the whole cervical spine (E). ( F-I) Bilateral transverse venous sinus stenosis. A patient in their 30s with right PT, chronic headaches and sporadic dizziness. (F) Coronal CTV shows narrowed appearance of bilateral transverse sinuses. (G) Sagittal FLAIR sequences show a flattened or partially empty sella (white arrow) (H) Axial contrast-enhanced MRI demonstrates bilateral transverse venous sinus stenoses (white arrows). (I) Right common carotid artery injection (frontal projection) demonstrates severe right transverse stenosis (white arrow) as well as a laterally projecting sigmoid diverticulum . (J-M) Sigmoid sinus diverticulum with bony dehiscence. A patient in their 60s with right PT and conductive hearing loss. (J) Coronal CT temporal bone shows marked osseous dehiscence of the right sigmoid plate (white arrow) extending to the right mastoid bone (red arrow), contiguous with the mastoid air cells due to a large sigmoid diverticulum (asterisk). (K) Coronal T1-weighted gadolinium-enhanced MRI demonstrating a lateral and anterior outpouching of the right sigmoid sinus (white arrow) consistent with a sigmoid diverticulum. (L) Coronal MIP of TRICKS MRA demonstrates a dominant right-sided venous system with a focal outpouching at the right sigmoid sinus, consistent with sigmoid diverticulum (white arrow). (M) DSA shows right sigmoid sinus outpouching. (N-Q) Jugular bulb diverticulum. A patient in their 20s presenting with right-sided PT. (N) Sagittal T1-weighted SPGR sequence demonstrates an outpouching at the tip of the jugular bulb consistent with a jugular bulb diverticulum (white arrow). (O) Axial ToF MRA brain demonstrates the dominant right venous system with a jugular bulb diverticulum (white arrow). (P) Axial CT temporal bone at the level of the mastoid bone demonstrates and enlarged jugular fossa with a soft tissue mass extending into the middle ear cavity, compatible with jugular bulb diverticulum with the (Q) the superior most aspect of the jugular bulb diverticulum (straight black arrow) contacting the stapes (curved arrow) at the level of the mesotympanum. Vascular Shunting Lesions The identification of vascular shunting lesions such as dural arteriovenous fistulas (DAVFs), is critical in patients presenting with pulsatile tinnitus (PT), as these lesions can be associated with significant morbidity and increased risk of stroke. MRI with and without contrast with time of flight (ToF) MRA imaging is superior to CT and other modalities in sensitivity for DAVFs and is instrumental in initial evaluation for patients with PT[ 11 , 23 ]. Characteristic findings of a DAVF on MRA include abnormal vessels, dilated feeding arteries with enlarged draining veins, dilated cortical veins, and even venous sinus thrombosis. T2/FLAIR sequence can show hyperintensity in the area of the white matter where the DAVF is located, due to venous stasis. The ToF MRA sequence without contrast is particularly adept at delineating the hemodynamics of DAVFs (such as flow related enhancement of the sinus) and can provide detailed insight into the location of the shunting lesion and determination of the feeding artery and receiving venous pouch[ 17 ]. More recently, Arterial Spin Labeling (ASL) has also been found to be useful at identifying small shunting lesions even without the use of contrast, and especially in identifying small DAVFs that are more difficult to perceive on post-contrast sequences or ToF MRA sequences[ 7 ]. The presence of cortical venous reflux can elevate a DAVF to a high-risk lesion that requires treatment to prevent intracranial hemorrhage. Cortical venous reflux is not always reliably seen on MRI and in these cases DSA will be required as part of the diagnostic and management pipeline [ 24 ] In addition to intracranial DAVFs, extracranial dAVFs, such as those involving the cervical spine, can also lead to symptoms of pulsatile tinnitus. MRA of the neck using Time Resolved Imaging of Contrast KineticS (TRICKS) sequences can be used to identify these lesions and has been suggested to be a critical sequence in an institution’s comprehensive MRI protocol for pulsatile tinnitus [ 7 ]. Early venous opacification with opacification of abnormal arteriovenous connections seen on TRICKS in the presence of a DAVF. Since pulsatile tinnitus can come from a DAVF in the head or cervical spine, coverage of TRICKS sequences should include both the head and the cervical spine ( Figure 2 ). Similarly, ToF MRA sequences without contrast should also cover both the head and neck. While MRI is fundamental in the diagnosis of DAVFs, Computed Tomography Angiography (CTA) also plays a complementary role particularly in resource-poor settings, or in the emergent setting, or in settings where MRI is contraindicated. On CTA, the appearance of asymmetrically enlarged arterial feeding vessels were found to have a sensitivity of 86% for DAVFs, in addition to secondary signs of shaggy appearance of the dural venous sinus, transcalvarial venous channels and asymmetric venous collaterals[ 25 ]. However it should be noted that CTA is not sensitive for small DAVFs and if there is persistent clinical concern for the cause of PT, MRI should be performed. In cases of clinical concern for a DAVF, even if an MRI/MRA is normal, DSA should be performed. Clinically concerning symptoms include improvement of PT with occipital artery compression, higher-pitched pulse synchronous sound, additional neurologic deficits or symptoms other than visual field cuts, or a focal neurologic deficit (such as a cranial neuropathy). Carotid duplex ultrasound has also shown to be 95% sensitive for DAVFs when resistive indices (RI) for the external carotid artery or the occipital artery are <0.7 [ 26 ]. More specifically, decreased RI and increased end diastolic volume (EDV) of the ECA has been shown to have >90% sensitivity in diagnosing DAVFs when the ECA or a branch of the ECA is a feeding artery of the DAVF [ 27 ]. In general, while ultrasound may be helpful as an inexpensive means of evaluating for carotid stenosis and atherosclerosis and potentially (but practically challenging) for monitoring the DAVF in the setting of non-operative management, it is limited by its inability to localize and characterize the fistula, necessitating follow-up with MRI/MRA for complete evaluation. In a routine practice setting, sonographers trained to complete vascular ultrasounds of the neck are well-versed in obtaining resistive indices of the common carotid arteries and the internal carotid arteries. In tertiary care settings, obtaining resistive indices of the ECA may also be routine, and commonly performed. To incorporate ECA RI measurements into existing ultrasound protocols, extra training may be required for sonographers to identify the appropriate anatomic location of the ECA and using Doppler to calculate RIs, although this would not differ significantly from taking other RI measurements. However, our experience has shown the operator dependence nature of ultrasound can make following DAVF patients long term a challenging with this modality. High-Risk Arterial Pathologies Arterial lesions such as aneurysms, arteriovenous malformations (AVMs), cervical atherosclerotic disease, and dissection pose a significant clinical risk in the etiology of pulsatile tinnitus (PT), with the potential to lead to serious cerebrovascular events. The detection and characterization of such lesions are hence of paramount clinical importance. ToF MRA is highly effective in diagnosing aneurysms, which may present with PT due to altered hemodynamics and turbulent blood flow[ 28 ] due to the presence of the aneurysm sac. Similarly, TRICKS and post-contrast T1 sequence imaging is beneficial for visualizing AVMs, and identifying the characteristic feeding arterial vasculature, nidus and draining vein. In addition to identifying the vasculature involved in the AVM, SWI (Susceptibility Weighted Imaging) can be used to identify hemorrhage within the AVM. In studies by Machet et al[ 29 ], there was a significant correlation between MRI using TRICKS and the subsequent DSA on identifying AVMs and the corresponding nidus, although not all arterial feeders may be identifiable. The resolution of DSA is superior to MRI in characterizing the complex architecture of the lesion, however, MRI can be an important tool for radiation treatment planning and follow up. In cases where MRI is not available, a single-phase CT arteriovenous phase (where imaging is acquired 40 seconds after the contrast bolus), has been shown to identify AVMs given, although limited in the case of small AVMs[ 4 ]. Carotid-cavernous fistulas (CCFs), characterized by shunting between the carotid arterial system and the cavernous sinus, are commonly seen in the setting of ruptured cavernous ICA aneurysm or now (courtesy of advances in airbags and seatbelts) more rarely trauma. Typical characteristics of CCFs on MRA or CTA include early enhancement of the cavernous sinus with dilated draining veins such as the superior ophthalmic vein[ 30 ]. Steno-occlusive atherosclerotic disease (ACD) of the neck involving vessels such as the carotid arteries, vertebral arteries, including their origins at the aortic arch ( Figure 3 ) can present with PT, especially in patients with relevant risk factors (such as hypertension, etc), infarct on MRI, or in older populations. In such populations, some studies have shown that atherosclerotic disease as the most common cause of arterial tinnitus as diagnosed by CT angiography (CTA) [ 31 ]. Carotid ultrasounds are often used as part of the screening protocol for atherosclerotic disease, but MRA of the neck can be included as part of a comprehensive screening MRI exam [ 7 ]. If a unilateral bruit is present in the setting of PT, atherosclerosis of the ECA was found to be the most common cause of PT on ultrasound [ 32 ]. In a general cohort of patients with PT, carotid doppler ultrasound identified ACD as the cause of unilateral tinnitus in 12% of patients [ 33 ]. However, in a meta-analysis by Saxena et al[ 34 ], MRA of the neck was found to have higher sensitivity than CTA and carotid ultrasound in identifying stenotic lesions of the cervical vasculature. The TRICKS sequence can also be used to identify flow abnormalities that are present in the setting of stenoses. All patients with imaging findings of an acute or subacute stroke coincident with their pulsatile tinnitus should have a full evaluation of the arteries for a source that extends from the aorta to the location of the infarct. If the non-invasive imaging is unrevealing, DSA is recommended. Figure 3: Arterial causes of pulsatile tinnitus, which increase the risk of stroke. (A-C) Brachiocephalic artery stenosis. Open in a new tab A patient in their 50s presenting with right PT. (A) Coronal MIP and (B) axial MIP of CT angiography of the neck shows a critical stenosis of the origin of right brachiocephalic artery due to calcific atherosclerosis (white arrows). (C) DSA of the aorta shows severe narrowing of the right brachiocephalic artery at its origin (yellow arrow) prior to percutaneous balloon angioplasty. (D-G) Fibromuscular dysplasia. A patient in their 70s with history of left greater than right PT. (D) Coronal MIP of TRICKS MRA neck demonstrates tortuosity and irregularly irregular caliber of the high right and left internal carotid arteries (white arrows). (E) Sagittal MIP reformat of TRICKS MRA demonstrates normal appearance of the venous structures. (F) Coronal MIP of TRICKS MRA and (G) shows irregular beaded appearance of the right internal and external carotid arteries (white arrows). In the context of PT, cervical arterial dissection is another critical condition that must be ruled out, especially when accompanied by acute neck pain or symptoms of stroke. On post-contrast T1 weighted images with fat saturation, the characteristic finding of an intramural hematoma associated with a dissection flap can be seen as T1 shortening[ 18 ]. 3D SPGR can also show irregularity of the wall with an intimal flap or double lumen[ 7 ]. When compared to conventional angiography however, CTA had the highest sensitivity to dissections in the setting of PT when compared to MRA or carotid duplex ultrasound [ 35 ]. It is important to obtain these imaging studies as soon as possible, and consider placing the patient on an anti-platelet medication such as aspirin. Finally, fibromuscular dysplasia (FMD), which can also be seen in the setting of PT, is characterized by the classic ‘string of beads’ appearance and can also lead to extracranial narrowing of the carotid arteries ( Figure 3 ). FMD can also predispose patients to dissection, aneurysm formation and can lead to stroke, which underscores the importance of searching for this etiology in the emergent clinical setting. In the senior author’s personal experience of treating PT patients, FMD is most commonly located in the high cervical internal carotid artery and must cause significant stenosis to cause PT. The external carotid artery has also been reported to be a location for FMD in patients with PT [ 36 ]. The TRICKS sequence can be utilized for visualizing the alternating stenosis and dilation appearance of the cervical vasculature which creates the “string of beads” appearance[ 7 ]. Low-risk venous etiologies For objective PT, the most commonly identified cause is venous in etiology whether investigated using MRI with MRV or dual phase CT [ 2 , 5 , 9 , 37 , 38 ]. A venous cause of PT can be suspected clinically when tinnitus is mitigated by the Valsalva maneuver or manual compression of the ipsilateral internal jugular vein [ 13 ]. In addition to venous sinus stenosis, which is the most common cause of PT in the absence of IIH, other venous causes of PT include enlarged emissary and condylar veins, jugular bulb abnormalities, sigmoid sinus diverticula (which are commonly associated with venous sinus stenosis, and likely caused by the stenosis)[ 39 ]. Characteristic findings of venous sinus stenosis include narrowing of the transverse or sigmoid sinuses, most commonly. Enlarged emissary veins which include the posterior condylar emissary vein, the mastoid emissary vein and petrosquamosal vein are usually associated with a stenotic jugular vein or hypoplastic sigmoid sinus[ 4 ] and can lead to turbulent flow adjacent to the cochlea and manifest PT. These enlarged veins can be appreciated on post-contrast T1 sequences with fat saturation[ 2 ] or if particularly prominent, on a dual phase CT-AV[ 4 ]. Doppler ultrasound can also be applied to the mastoid bone of the side of tinnitus to appreciate turbulent flow through an enlarged emissary vein [ 40 ]. Jugular bulb abnormalities, such as a high-riding jugular bulb, are a common cause of venous PT ( Figure 2 )[ 30 ]. Studies using computational fluid dynamics (CFD) modeling using 2D-MRV and contrast-enhanced MRA show that a helical flow pattern in the proximal jugular vein in the setting of a high-riding jugular bulb can lead to perception of tinnitus[ 41 ]. A high-riding jugular bulb is identified when the superior most aspect of the jugular bulb extends more cranially than the petrous temporal bone. 3D and MRV sequences can be used to identify jugular bulb abnormalities[ 7 ]. CT imaging is also useful for detecting a high-riding jugular bulb and is superior in detecting bony erosion associated with a jugular diverticulum as well as dehiscence of the jugular bulb[ 30 , 42 ]. Sigmoid sinus diverticula, are best diagnosed with post-contrast T1 weighted sequences, 3D SPGR or MRV[ 7 ]as a lateral outpouching of the sigmoid sinus ( Figure 2 ). Moreover, sigmoid sinus diverticula are usually found with concomitant ipsilateral transverse or sigmoid sinus stenoses. CFD analysis from 4D Flow MRI can identify turbulent flow and high-velocity jets from this concomitant stenosis to the diverticulum [ 39 ]. 4D Flow MRI is also beneficial for planning stent placement[ 43 ]. Low-risk arterial etiologies Several benign arterial pathologies can present with PT, including congenital anomalies such as an aberrant course of the internal carotid artery, or a persistent stapedial artery. It is unclear how a patient with a congenital anomaly develops symptoms in their third or fourth decade of life, but perhaps the relatively new onset of symptoms from these congenital anomalies is related to atherosclerotic depositions (too small to be seen on angiography or vessel wall imaging) causing turbulence. An aberrant course of the internal carotid artery (ICA) occurs in the setting of congenital aplasia of an extracranial ICA with an arterial collateral in place. While MRA can be used to identify an aberrant internal carotid artery, it may be better appreciated on high resolution CT when using the bony landmarks to show the anomalous lateral course of the aberrant ICA through the temporal bone[ 30 ]. In case of a persistent stapedial artery, which is difficult to see on MRA given its small caliber, CTA is preferred. On CTA, the persistent stapedial artery can be diagnosed using indirect signs such as the absence of the foramen spinosum and the bony enlargement of the tympanic facial nerve segment[ 44 ]. In addition, a persistent stapedial artery is suspected in the setting of PT and if a retrotympanic mass is seen on otologic examination[ 45 ]. PT can also be experienced in the setting of neurovascular compression syndromes. Vascular loops predominantly of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) located at the cerebellopontine angle can impinge on the vestibulocochlear nerve, and can be best identified with MRI Fast Imaging Employing Steady State Acquisition (FIESTA) sequences or post-contrast T1 weighted sequences [ 18 ]. Neoplasms In the diagnostic algorithm for PT, ruling out tumors is of critical importance, given the implications for patient management and prognosis. In the setting of potential neoplastic etiologies, MRI’s superiority in soft tissue contrast resolution is invaluable for early and accurate detection and has been shown to be preferred in diagnosing neoplastic origins of unilateral PT [ 46 ]. Vestibular schwannomas, which often also present with asymmetric sensorineural hearing loss in addition to PT, present as a small focus of enhancement within the cerebellopontine angle or the inner ear in MRI T1-weighted post-contrast sequences or FIESTA sequences[ 13 , 47 ]. Intracochlear schwannomas, a rare subset of 8th nerve schwannomas, require MR, where the mass presents as a small avidly enhancing T1 hyperintense and T2 hypointense mass in the region of the cochlea ( Figure 4 ). Additional 3D SPGR sequences have also been shown effective in identifying intracochlear schwannomas[ 7 ]. Figure 4: Neoplastic and structural causes of pulsatile tinnitus. (A-C) Intracochlear Schwannoma. Open in a new tab A patient in their 30s with history of right PT and sensorineural hearing loss. (A) Axial T1-weighted gadolinium-enhanced fat saturated MRI demonstrates linear enhancement (white arrow) at the basal turn of the right cochlea. (B) Axial T2-weighted FIESTA sequence demonstrates T2 hypointensity corresponding to area of enhancement at the basal turn of the cochlea in (A). (C) Coronal T1-weighted gadolinium-enhanced fat-saturated MRI demonstrates linear enhancement (white arrows) of the basal and middle turns of the right cochlea compared to the normal lack of enhancement on the left cochlea, compatible with intracochlear schwannoma. (D-G) Encephalocele, or brain herniation into an arachnoid granulation. A patient in their 40s with left sided pulsatile tinnitus. (D) Axial T2-weighted FIESTA sequence demonstrates mass-like T2 hypointensity (asterisk) within an arachnoid granulation (red outline). (E) Axial T1-weighted MRI demonstrating T1 isointense left temporal encephalocele (asterisk) extending into a prominent arachnoid granulation which narrows the left sigmoid sinus (white arrow). (F) Axial MIP of MRA shows narrowing of the non-dominant left sigmoid sinus (white arrow). (G) Axial T2-weighted FLAIR MRI demonstrates asymmetric left posterior temporal white matter extending into the left sigmoid sinus (black arrow). Glomus tympanicum tumors are small tumors situated in the cochlear promontory presenting as a small avidly enhancing mass on post-contrast T1-weighted sequences on MRI imaging[ 30 ]. Given the size of the tumor and its proclivity for bony involvement, CT of the temporal bone can also be used to determine extent of bone erosion in the middle ear cavity[ 4 , 18 ] and can specify the location of the tumor for surgical planning[ 30 ]. Jugular paragangliomas, or glomus jugulare tumors, are vascular tumors located in the jugular fossa and jugular foramen with a characteristic salt and pepper imaging appearance on T1 and T2 weighted sequences, with avid enhancement on post-contrast and MRA sequences[ 30 , 44 ]. The extent of bony erosion by jugular paragangliomas can be determined by CT[ 44 ]. Other less common neoplastic causes of PT include hemangiopericytomas, vascular metastases, meningiomas, and endolymphatic sac tumors, which can be all be effectively identified on MRI with and without contrast, as they typically exhibit avid enhancement[ 4 , 48 ]. Given the diversity of neoplastic conditions that may present with PT, MRI stands out as the preferred initial modality due to its comprehensive and non-ionizing nature, while offering a baseline for surgical planning. However, in patients with hearing loss who do not have an intracochlear schwannoma, a CT temporal bone is recommended to identify bony abnormalities that can also cause hearing loss. Bony abnormalities In evaluating for osseous causes of PT, CT is superior to MRI. Sigmoid sinus wall abnormalities including sigmoid sinus wall dehiscence (SSWD) and sigmoid plate dehiscence has a significant correlation with PT[ 38 , 49 ] and also IIH[ 50 ] and was identified in up to 70% of patients with PT when evaluated by CT[ 51 ]. While sigmoid sinus wall dehiscence can be seen in non-PT patients, a higher grade of wall dehiscence is usually symptomatic in PT patients[ 52 , 53 ]. Often, sigmoid sinus wall abnormalities are seen in patients with a sigmoid sinus diverticulum and assessing for this abnormality in conjunction with wall dehiscence. Sigmoid sinus wall abnormalities are also often accompanied by transverse sinus stenosis, highlighting the importance of scrutinizing the adjacent vasculature on CTV[ 42 ]. Moreover, more than one sigmoid plate dehiscence has also been found to be associated with ipsilateral venous flow dominance and a high-riding jugular bulb[ 54 ]. Enlarged vestibular aqueducts can be seen in the setting of PT and are more easily diagnosed on temporal bone CTs, although MRI can also be used[ 7 , 14 ]. The vestibular aqueduct is a narrow bony canal that connects the inner ear to the endolymphatic sac and contains the endolymphatic duct. The vestibular aqueduct is hypothesized to regulate the ionic balance within the inner ear. Generally, enlarged vestibular aqueducts are seen congenitally, in the setting of sensorineural hearing loss. Per the Cincinnati criteria, vestibular aqueducts measuring greater than 1.0 mm are considered enlarged [ 55 ]. A good internal control is the superior semicircular canal; the vestibular aqueduct should not be larger than the superior semicircular canal. It should be noted enlarged vestibular aqueducts can occur bilaterally, and symmetry cannot be used to conclude normalcy. Superior semicircular canal dehiscence is also a cause of PT and can also be seen in the setting of IIH due to remodeling of the skull base. Often difficult to see on MRI, and temporal bone CT is required to appreciate the characteristic thinning of the bone overlying the superior semicircular canal[ 31 ]. Otosclerosis or otospongiosis can also be a cause of PT, and is commonly seen in women with associated hearing loss [ 44 , 45 , 48 ]. Hypodensity within the fissulae antefenestrum is the most common finding of fenestral otospongiosis [ 48 ]. Less common conditions such as fibrous dysplasia and Paget’s disease can also result in symptomatic PT. For both of these fibro-osseous pathologies, involvement of the temporal bone can be characterized by CT temporal bone. Fibrous dysplasia is characterized by an expansile lesion of the bone with well-defined borders affecting the temporal bone in the setting of tinnitus, and is best seen on CT. Paget disease, which can lead to PT through involvement of the temporal bone beyond the otic capsule and presents with extensive demineralization and subsequent sclerotic thickening, is more precisely characterized using CT [ 45 ]. Other structural pathologies In the setting of an abnormal audiology examination and hearing loss in addition to PT where initial MRI is unremarkable (and tumors such as intracochlear schwannomas have been ruled out), CT should be considered for further examination[ 13 ]. Cholesteatoma, which is a collection of squamous tissue which develops in the middle ear, can be congenital or acquired in the setting of chronic otitis media. Cholesteatoma can result in PT when it compresses the sigmoid sinus leading to the audiologic perception of turbulent flow. Best demonstrated on CT [ 45 ], characteristic findings of a cholesteatoma include soft tissue in the epitympanum within Prussak’s space (lateral to the ossicles), with associated erosive changes of the scutum and middle ear ossicles. Encephaloceles, which can be congenital or result from trauma, infection, surgery, or IIH [ 16 ], are effectively characterized using contrast-enhanced T2-weighted MRI imaging or 3D SPGR sequences[ 7 , 14 ]. Encephaloceles appear as low-signal lesions protruding through bony dehiscence, surrounded by cerebrospinal fluid signal intensity[ 56 ] or on CT as a soft tissue density structure protruding through the epitympanum and resting on the ossicles. Notably, brain herniations into arachnoid granulations (BHAG), are frequently observed in patients with IIH. Smith et al[ 57 ] describe the diagnosis of these specific types of encephaloceles on post-contrast 3D SPGR sequences, where they typically present as herniations into the transverse or transverse-sigmoid junctions ( Figure 4 ). Conclusion As imaging modalities have become more sophisticated, the sensitivity and specificity for identifying the cause of pulsatile tinnitus using non-invasive imaging techniques has significantly increased. The advent of new MRI sequences such as TRICKS, ASL, and 4D Flow has allowed neurointerventionalists to effectively identify dangerous, treatable causes of pulsatile tinnitus and plan either conservative or interventional treatments in advance or in place of conventional angiography. Decreasing reliance on digital subtraction angiography to accurately diagnose the cause of PT also allows for decreased healthcare costs and reduced radiation dose to the patient. In this review, we discuss the advantages of using MRI a first-line non-invasive imaging modality to first rule out the most dangerous causes of PT requiring treatment and also appreciate the low-risk etiologies for which conservative management can be pursued. Several reviews in the past have addressed discussed the imaging findings of PT using exclusively CT/CTA/CTV[ 4 , 30 , 31 ], however these reviews acknowledge limitations in identifying causes such as tumors, IIH and DAVFs. CT and ultrasound remain important complementary imaging modalities, especially for osseous etiologies of PT or for monitoring in low-risk vascular lesions of the neck. CT and ultrasound remain critical non-invasive imaging modalities to diagnose PT in emergent settings, low-resource settings or when MRI is contraindicated. Supplementary Material Supp1 NIHMS2031067-supplement-Supp1.pdf (57.7KB, pdf) Funding: This study was funded by the National Institute of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering (T32EB001631). Footnotes Ethics Approval: This study does not include human participants, and thus an IRB is not required. This manuscript does not require Ethics Approval as it is a narrative review and does not include identifiable patient data. The authors declare no competing interests. References 1. Amans M, Mattay R, Hills NK, et al. More than just noise: Association of pulsatile tinnitus with anxiety, depression, and reduction of quality of life. Interv Neuroradiol. 2023;15910199231168751. doi: 10.1177/15910199231168751 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Abdalkader M, Nguyen TN, Norbash AM, et al. State of the Art: Venous Causes of Pulsatile Tinnitus and Diagnostic Considerations Guiding Endovascular Therapy. Radiology. 2021;300:2–16. doi: 10.1148/radiol.2021202584 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Shweel M, Hamdy B. Diagnostic utility of magnetic resonance imaging and magnetic resonance angiography in the radiological evaluation of pulsatile tinnitus. American Journal of Otolaryngology. 2013;34:710–7. doi: 10.1016/j.amjoto.2013.08.001 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Kumar R, Rice S, Lingam RK. Detecting causes of pulsatile tinnitus on CT arteriography-venography: A pictorial review. European Journal of Radiology. 2021;139:109722. doi: 10.1016/j.ejrad.2021.109722 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Formeister EJ, Xiao G, Clark J, et al. Combined Arterial and Venous Phase Computed Tomographic Imaging of the Skull Base in Pulsatile Tinnitus. Otol Neurotol. 2022;43:1049–55. doi: 10.1097/MAO.0000000000003672 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Cao AC, Hwa TP, Cavarocchi C, et al. Diagnostic Yield and Utility of Radiographic Imaging in the Evaluation of Pulsatile Tinnitus: A Systematic Review. Otology & Neurotology Open. 2023;3:e030. doi: 10.1097/ONO.0000000000000030 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Cummins DD, Caton MT, Shah V, et al. MRI and MR angiography evaluation of pulsatile tinnitus: A focused, physiology-bas